etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Isis}} [[File:Ägyptischer Maler um 1360 v. Chr. 001.jpg|thumb|<hiero>st-t-B1</hiero>wall painting circa 1360 BC]]

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈʀuːsat|m=ˈʀuːsaʔ|ml=ˈʔuːsa|l=ˈʔeːsə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 2===
{{anchor|feeble}}
Faulkner suggests this word may be a corrupt writing of {{m|egy|ꜣhd||to be(come) weak; to quiver}}; Erman and Grapow instead suggest a connection with {{m|egy|jꜣd||to suffer}}.

===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|egy|Old Egyptian}} {{IPAfont|/Vj/}}, where V represents an unknown short vowel.
{{egy-IPA-E}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>y</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Marks the [[dual]] form of nouns and adjectives. Attaches to the singular form if feminine or the plural form if masculine.}}
# {{ng|Sometimes added to [[suffix]] [[pronoun]]s attached to [[dual]] [[noun]]s.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
<div>Often this suffix is represented by writing the [[phonetic]] or [[determinative]] glyph twice, e.g. <hiero>N16:N16</hiero> for {{m|egy|tꜣwj}}.</div>

=====Derived terms=====
{{col2|egy
|-wj
|-tj}}

===Etymology 2===
Hypothesized to be from {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*-i|pos=genitive-possessive case ending}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ij|m=ij|l=(ej)
|l2={{q|stressed}} {{IPA|egy|/ej/}} or {{IPA|egy|/eʔ/}}; {{q|unstressed}} silent, but preserves root-final {{IPA|egy|/t/}} or {{IPA|egy|/ɾ/}} that would otherwise be lost}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>y</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Converts nouns (including proper nouns and ''[[nisba]]''s used nominally), prepositions, independent pronouns, and numerals into masculine adjectives: the masculine ''[[nisba]]'' adjective ending.}}

=====Usage notes=====
In Old Egyptian this suffix was still [[productive]]. By Late Egyptian this was no longer the case, and adjectives with the suffix were [[lexicalize]]d.

=====Alternative forms=====
This suffix is sometimes not written; in Old Egyptian, such omission is the usual practice.

{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i</hiero>|read1=-j|date1=Old Egyptian
|<hiero>i-i</hiero>|read2=-y|date2=Old Egyptian}}

===Etymology 3===
Hypothesized to be from earlier {{m|egy||*-iu}} or {{m|egy||*-iju}}, from {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*-u|pos=nominative case ending}} attached to a root ending in {{m|afa-pro||*-i}} or {{m|afa-pro||*-ij}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ij|m=ij|l=(ej)
|l2={{q|stressed}} {{IPA|egy|/ej/}} or {{IPA|egy|/eʔ/}}; {{q|unstressed}} silent, but preserves root-final {{IPA|egy|/t/}} or {{IPA|egy|/ɾ/}} that would otherwise be lost}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms i-stem masculine nouns from roots.}}

===Etymology 5===
From the earlier infinitival ending {{m|egy|-t}}; the consonant of this suffix became silent over time, leaving its remaining vowel to be represented by {{m|egy||-j}}.

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>y</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[infinitive]] of anomalous verbs, weak verbs (except for fourth weak verbs with a geminated stem), and causative biliteral verbs.}}

===Etymology 6===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>y</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine imperfective active participle, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i-i</hiero>|read1=-y}}

===Etymology 7===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>y</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine geminated perfective passive participle of strong biliteral verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

