etymologies.etymology_text:
===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|egy|id=plant}}
{{unc|egy}} etymology. With different determinatives the word is associated with the soles of a human foot or the hoof of an animal, perhaps suggesting a correlation to its known anti-fungal properties, anethole still being used to treat athlete's foot and thrush.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*ʔanāku}}. {{surf|egy|jn|.k|pos1= independent pronoun morpheme|pos2= first person stative ending}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=jaˈnak|m=jaˈnak|ml=ʔaˈnak|l=ʔaˈnak|ref=<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|31, 33, 44, 65}}</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Perhaps from a form such as {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*waqar-}}; compare {{cog|he|יָקָר|tr=yāqār|t=expensive, precious}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|q=noun|o=ˈjaːqʼaɾ|m=ˈjaːqʼaʔ|ml=ˈjaːqʼa|l=ˈjoːqʼə}}
{{egy-IPA-R|q=adjective|o=ˈjaːqʼiɾ|m=ˈjaːqʼiʔ|ml=ˈjaːqʼa|l=ˈjoːqʼə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2023|August|19}}

===Etymology===
Perhaps compare {{m|egy|qḥ||to break stones}}, {{m|egy|qḥqḥ||to hammer}}, if not {{m|egy|jqḥ||to enter, to illuminate}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|jqḥ_w}}

