etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|jt|gloss1=father|nṯrw}}, plural of {{m|egy|nṯr||god}}, in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Father of the Gods’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˌjatijnaˈcuːɾaw|m=ˌjatijnaˈtuːɾaw|ml=ˌʔatənəˈtuːɾə|l=ˌʔatənəˈteːɾ}}
{{egy-IPA-E|jt-nṯr_w}}

===Etymology 1===

====Prefix====
{{egy-prefix|head=<hiero>i</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|chiefly|Old Egyptian|and|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Forms the imperative of biliteral verbs, and in Late Egyptian also of former triliteral and third-weak verbs that have been reduced to two radicals. Optional in Late Egyptian.}}
# {{lb|egy|chiefly|Old Egyptian|and|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Forms the subjunctive of biliteral verbs.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i-A2</hiero>|read1=j.|date1=Late Egyptian}}

===Etymology 2===

====Prefix====
{{egy-prefix|head=<hiero>i-A2</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Forms the participles and relative forms of [[fientive]] (non-adjectival) biliteral verbs, triliteral and third-weak verbs that have been reduced to biliterals through sound change, fourth-weak verbs, and a few other triliteral verbs.}} {{defdate|since the 19th Dynasty}}

=====Usage notes=====
This prefix is often unwritten when preceded by articles or demonstratives. It is rarely used with the verb {{m|egy|wnn||to exist}}.

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i-w</hiero>|read1=jw.
|<hiero>r</hiero>|read2=r.}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2019|October|21}}

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|j.ḫm|t1=not knowing|pos1=imperfective active participle of {{m|egy|ḫm}}|wrḏ|t2=to be(come) weary}}, thus literally ‘(one) not knowing wearying’, because noncircumpolar stars circle in long paths without end.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2019|February|27}}

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|j.ḫm|t1=not knowing|pos1=imperfective active participle of {{m|egy|ḫm}}|skj|alt2=sk|t2=destruction}}, thus literally ‘(one) not knowing destruction’, because the circumpolar stars never set and so were considered imperishable.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 1===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-i</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Forms the [[plural]] [[imperative]] of verbs, particularly [[weak verb]]s.}}
# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally used to form the [[subjunctive]] of [[weak verb]]s.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The plural imperative can also appear without this suffix, in a form identical to the singular imperative.

===Etymology 3===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-i</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine imperfective active participle, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>y</hiero>|read1=-j}}

===Etymology 4===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-i</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine singular perfective passive participle of strong verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>W</hiero>|read1=-w
|<hiero>w</hiero>|read2=-w}}

===Etymology 5===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-i</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally marks the perfective passive participle of weak verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}
# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally marks the perfective relative form of weak verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}
# {{lb|egy|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Marks the perfective passive participle of mostly third-weak transitive verbs, following the stem.}}
# {{lb|egy|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Occasionally marks the perfective active participle of mostly third-weak transitive verbs, following the stem.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i-i-A</hiero>|read1=-yꜣ|note1=Late Egyptian
|<hiero>i-i-t</hiero>|read2=-yt|note2=Late Egyptian}}

===Etymology 6===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-i</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Rarely marks the imperfective relative form of all verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>W</hiero>|read1=-w
|<hiero>w</hiero>|read2=-w}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
The first consonant {{m|egy||y}} presumably derives from the final (weak) radical {{m|egy||j}} of a fourth weak verb in contact with the suffix {{m|egy|-w}}, which forms the second consonant of {{m|egy||-yw}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Suffix===
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-i-w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[negatival complement]] of fourth weak verbs.}}

====Usage notes====
The ordinary ending {{m|egy|-w}} for the negatival complement is also used with fourth weak verbs.

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|174}}

