etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ꜥšꜣ|t1=to be(come) numerous|r|alt2=r(ꜣ)|t2=mouth, utterance}}, literally ‘to be(come) numerous of mouths/utterances’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ʕiˌʃiʀˈɾaʀ/|a=Old Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

ꜥkꜣ.references:
* {{R:egy:Ahituv|48|entry=Acco}}
* Simons, S. J. (1937) ''Handbook for the Study of Egyptian Topographical Lists Relating to Western Asia'', pages 137, 139
* {{R:egy:LÄ|1|116–117|head=Akko}}

{{C|egy|Cities|Cities in Canaan}}

etymologies.etymology_text:
===Etymology 1===
Possibly derived from a lengthened form of {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*-u||pos=nominative case marker}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=aw/, /w|m=aw/, /w|l=(ə)/, /ʔ/, /w
|onote=, sometimes with ablaut; specific nouns used either one form of the suffix, the other, or both in free variation
|mnote=, as in Old Egyptian
|l2={{IPA|egy|/(ə)/|/ʔ/}}, respectively, but {{IPA|egy|/w/}} in consonant stem nouns (often metathesized to precede the preceding consonant), all sometimes with ablaut or further changes; regularity is lost}}
{{egy-IPA-E|-_u}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{non-gloss|Used to form the [[masculine]] [[plural]] forms of [[noun]]s and [[adjective]]s}}

=====Usage notes=====
The plural is occasionally represented by writing the [[phonetic]] or [[determinative]] glyph three times, e.g. {{m|egy|tꜣw}}: <hiero>N16:N16:N16</hiero>

=====Derived terms=====
{{col2|egy
|-wj
|-wt}}

====See also====
* {{l|egy|𓅱}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*-u|pos=nominative case marker}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=w|m=w|l=(w)
|l2=usually lost, but {{IPA|egy|/w/}} after a stressed vowel}}
{{egy-IPA-E|-_u}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms a-stem masculine nouns from roots.}}
#: {{m|egy||ḥfꜣ-}} → {{m|egy|ḥfꜣw||(male) snake}}
# {{ng|Forms u-stem masculine nouns from roots.}}
#: {{m|egy||hrw-}} → {{m|egy|hrw|hrw(w)|day}}

=====Usage notes=====
In good orthography, {{m|egy||-w}} is written with a-stem nouns but left unwritten with u-stem nouns, hence {{m|egy||hrww}}, {{m|egy||rꜥw}} are written {{m|egy|hrw}}, {{m|egy|rꜥ}}.

===Etymology 4===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms adverbs from certain adjectives}}; [[-ly]]
# {{ng|Forms [[w:Prepositional adverb|prepositional adverbs]] from certain prepositions.}}

===Etymology 5===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[negatival complement]] of all verbs except geminate and anomalous verbs.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Often this ending is entirely omitted.

===Etymology 6===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[prospective]] of all verbs except second geminate, strong bi- and triliteral, and (usually) anomalous verbs.}}
# {{ng|Forms the [[prospective]] [[passive]] of all verbs except fourth weak, strong bi- and triliteral, and (usually) anomalous verbs.}}
# {{ng|Forms the [[perfect]] [[passive]] of all verbs except second geminate verbs.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Often this ending is entirely omitted.

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i-i</hiero>|read1=-y|note1=chiefly in weak verbs}}

===Etymology 7===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine singular imperfective passive participle, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}
# {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine singular imperfective relative form of all verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>W</hiero>|read1=-w
|<hiero>i-i</hiero>|read2=-y|note2=relative form only; rare}}

===Etymology 8===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Optionally marks the masculine singular perfective passive participle of strong verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender/number endings.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>W</hiero>|read1=-w
|<hiero>i-i</hiero>|read2=-y}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
* Possibly from a form such as {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*wrḳ-}}.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|31–32}}</ref> If so, cognate with {{cog|sem-pro|*waraq-||green}} and {{cog|ber-pro|*wăraɣ}}. Although, compare {{m|egy|jꜣqt}}.

* Through Belova's Law; metathesized from ''*ꜣwḏ''. Related to {{cog|sem-pro|*rawḏ̣-|t=garden}}, whence {{cog|ar|رَوْضَة}}.<ref>Takács, G. (1998). "The law of Belova in work". ''Rocznik Orientalistyczny'', page 119</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|q=verb|o=ˈwaːʀacʼ|m=ˈwaːʀatʼ|ml=ˈwaːʔətʼ|l=ˈwoːʔətʼ}}
{{egy-IPA-R|q=adjective|o=ˈwaːʀicʼ|m=ˈwaːʀitʼ|ml=ˈwaːʔətʼ|l=ˈwoːʔətʼ
|onote=<ref name=Loprieno/>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{m|egy|-w||masculine plural ending}} + {{m|egy|-j||dual ending}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=wVj|m=wVj|ml=wə|l=w|V=1}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Suffix===
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w&y</hiero>}}

# {{non-gloss|Used to form [[regular]] [[masculine]] [[dual]] forms of [[noun]]s and [[adjective]]s}}
# {{non-gloss|Attaches to the adjective in an adjectival predicate to give its clause admirative exclamatory force:}} [[how|How]] [[very]]… ! [[how|How]]… !
#* {{Q|egy||GHtO.|line 21|quote=<hiero>bnr-w&y-U7:t:f-x:r:n:Z2</hiero>
|tr=bnr'''wj''' mr(w)t.f ḫr.n
|t='''How''' sweet is the love of him among us!}}

====Alternative forms====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>W:y</hiero>|read1=-wj
|<hiero>w</hiero>|read2=-w}}

The dual suffix is also often represented by writing the [[phonetic]] or [[determinative]] glyph twice, e.g. {{m|egy|tꜣwj}}: <hiero>N16:N16</hiero>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Loprieno|60}}
* {{R:egy:Allen|39–40, 70–71, 337}}

==Egyptian==
{{number box|egy|1}}

===Etymology===
Formed as if the perfective active participle of {{m|egy|wꜥj||to be(come) alone}}, although possibly the verb may be derived from the numeral instead.

===Pronunciation 1===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈwiʕjVw|m=ˈwiʕjVw|ml=ˈwiʕjə|l=ˈweʕi|mnote=<ref>{{cite-book|last=Callender|first=John|year=1975|title=Middle Egyptian|publisher=Undena Publications|page=56|text=wíꜥjaw}}</ref><ref>{{cite-journal|last=Edel|first=Elmar|title=Neues Material zur Herkunft der auslautenden Vokale -ⲉ und -ⲓ im Koptischen|journal=Zeitschrift für Ägyptische Sprache und Altertumskunde|year=1961|volume=86|issue=1|doi=10.1524/zaes.1961.86.1.107|passage=wĭꜥj˘w}}</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Compare {{m|egy|wꜥ||one, alone}}, either the source of the verb or a derivation of it.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˈwiʕjit/|a=Old Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈwiːʕiw|m=ˈwiːʕiw|ml=ˈwiːʕə|l=ˈweːʕ(ə)|ref=<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|39}}</ref>
|mlnote=; compare the Akkadian transcription {{m|akk|𒌑𒄿𒌑|tr=ú-i-ú}}
|lnote=; compare the Akkadian transcriptions {{m|akk|𒌑𒂊𒄴|tr=ú-e-eḫ}}, {{m|akk|𒌑𒂊𒂊|tr=ú-e-e}}, {{m|akk|𒌑𒂊𒌑|tr=ú-e-ú}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{number box|egy|1}}

===Etymology===
{{rdp|egy|wꜥ||one}}. The written form consists of {{m|egy|wꜥ||one}} + {{m|egy|zpwj snwj||twice, repeated}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|wꜥ|gloss1=one|nj|alt2=n(j)|gloss2=of}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Phrase===
{{head|egy|phrase|head=<hiero>T21:a:n</hiero>}}

# [[one]] ({{synonym of|egy|wꜥ}})
# {{lb|egy|colloquial}} [[a]], [[an]]

====Usage notes====
Unlike the bare adjective {{m|egy|wꜥ||one}}, which follows the noun it modifies, the phrase {{m|egy||wꜥ n(j)}} precedes it.

====Inflection====
{{egy-decl-adj|wꜥ nj, wꜥ n|wꜥt nt|-|-|-|-|nonotes=1}}

====Alternative forms====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>T21:a*W-Z1:n</hiero>|read1=wꜥ n}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|104}}

{{cln|egy|articles}}

wꜥḥ.references:
* {{R:egy:Wb|1|289.1–289.9}}
* {{R:egy:Faulkner|58}}
