etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Wepwawet}}

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|wpj|alt1=wp|t1=opener|wꜣwt|pos2=plural of {{m|egy|wꜣt||way}}}} in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘opener of the ways’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|wp-wꜣ_wt}}

===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈwapwut|m=ˈwapwuʔ|ml=ˈwapwa|l=ˈwapwə
|onote=<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|57}}<!--also p. 49, but Loprieno apparently made a typo */ˈwapwat/ there--></ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|wpt|pos1=infinitive of {{m|egy|wpj||to open}}|r|t2=mouth}}, thus ‘opening of the mouth’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=wVˈpaːwatɾaʀ|m=wVˈpaːtɾaʀ|ml=wəˈpaːʔɾə|l=wəˈpoːʔɾ|ref=<ref>Compare Gundacker, Roman (2018) “Retention or Rejection: The Fate of ‘Ältere Komposita’ at the Transition from the ‘Dreisilbengesetz’ to the ‘Zweisilbengesetz’” in ''Tradition and Transformation in Ancient Egypt'', page 160</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Pronunciation===
Based on descendant {{m|cop-sah|ⲟⲩⲱϥ}}:
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈwaːfaʀ|m=ˈwaːfaʀ|ml=ˈwaːfə|l=ˈwoːf}}
Based on descendant {{m|cop-sah|ⲟⲩⲟϥ}}:
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈwafʀuw|m=ˈwafʀuw|ml=ˈwafə|l=ˈwaf}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{unc|egy}}. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own. Alternatively, some authors<ref>Scheele-Schweitzer, Katrin (2007). “Zu den Königsnamen der 5. und 6. Dynastie” in ''Göttinger Miszellen''. Göttingen: Universität der Göttingen, Seminar für Agyptologie und Koptologie. 215: 91–94. ISSN 0344-385X</ref> have tentatively proposed to read it as {{m|egy||wn-js}}, from a form of {{m|egy|wnn||to exist}} + {{m|egy|js||intensifying particle}}, thus meaning ‘Who exists indeed’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˈwanjas/|a=Old Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2013|November|3}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|wnn|nfr|.w|alt3=(.w)|gloss1=(one who) continually exists|gloss2=to be good, perfect, beautiful|pos3=third-person masculine stative ending}}, thus literally ‘one who continually exists in being good/perfect/beautiful’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

