etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈwiːχiʀ|m=ˈwiːχiʀ|ml=ˈwiːχə|l=wiːχ}}{{attention|egy|Is this pronunciation supposed to be for the noun or the verb? What are the Coptic descendants?}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 1===
{{com|egy|-w|pos1=plural suffix|-t|pos2=feminine ending}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=wat|m=jaʔ/, /waʔ|ml=ʔə/, /wa|l=ʔ/, /wə
|m2={{IPAfont|/jaʔ/}} after a stressed vowel; {{IPAfont|/waʔ/}} otherwise
|ml2={{IPAfont|/ʔə/}} after a stressed vowel; {{IPAfont|/wa/}} otherwise
|l2={{IPAfont|/ʔ/}} after a stressed vowel; {{IPAfont|/wə/}} otherwise}}
{{egy-IPA-E|-_wt}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w*t</hiero>}}

# {{non-gloss|Used to form [[feminine]] [[plural]] forms of [[noun]]s and [[adjective]]s}}

=====Usage notes=====
<div>While generally written as simply {{m|egy|-t}} <hiero>t</hiero>, that this suffix represented {{m-self|egy|-wt}} is supported by both the Coptic evidence and occasional hieroglyphic writings.</div>

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>t</hiero>|read1=t}}

===Etymology 2===
Compare Hebrew {{m|he|־וּת||abstract/collective nouns-forming suffix|tr=-út}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=wat|m=jaʔ/, /waʔ|ml=ʔə/, /wa|l=ʔ/, /wə
|m2={{IPAfont|/jaʔ/}} after a stressed vowel; {{IPAfont|/waʔ/}} otherwise
|ml2={{IPAfont|/ʔə/}} after a stressed vowel; {{IPAfont|/wa/}} otherwise
|l2={{IPAfont|/ʔ/}} after a stressed vowel; {{IPAfont|/wə/}} otherwise}}
{{egy-IPA-E|-_wt}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w*t</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms masculine collective nouns.}}
#: {{m|egy|sbꜣ||star}} → {{m|egy|sbꜣwt||constellation}}

===Etymology 3===

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w*t</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[complementary infinitive]] of weak verbs.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Weak verbs can alternatively take the endings {{m|egy|-t}} or {{m|egy|-yt}} to form the complementary infinitive.

==Egyptian==

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|-_wtj}}

===Suffix===
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>w-t:y</hiero>}}

# {{form of|egy|Old Egyptian form|-tj|t=prospective participle ending}} {{ng|for final-weak verbs, causatives, and verbs with more than three radicals}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|332}}

bꜣ.references:
* {{R:egy:Faulkner}}
* van Sittert, Bianca (2020) ''“Maintaining Order over Chaos”: A study of the ba and baw concepts in the Predynastic Period, Early Dynastic Period, and Old Kingdom [Master’s Thesis, the American University in Cairo]. AUC Knowledge Fountain.
* Žabkar, L. V. (1968) ''A study of the Ba concept in ancient Egyptian texts''. Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 34. Chicago: University of Chicago Press
* Wolf-Brinkmann, E. M. (1968) ''Versuch einer Deutung des Begriffes ‘''bA''’ anhand der Überlieferung der Frühzeit und des Alten Reiches'', Freiburg i. Br.: G. Seeger

{{C|egy|Felids|Sheep|Male animals|Religion}}

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Bastet}}

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|bꜣst|t1=Bubastis|-t|pos2=feminine ''[[nisba]]'' ending}}.<ref>{{R:egy:EDE|2|86}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=buˈʀistit|m=buˈʀistiʔ|ml=ʔuˈbesta|l=ʔuˈβestə
|onote=<ref>Osing, Jürgen (1976) ''Die Nominalbildung des Ägyptischen'', pages 310, 376, 855–856; Osing reconstructs *b(u)ꜣést˘t. The value of the final vowel is determined from its origin as a feminine ''[[nisba]]'' ending.</ref>
|mlnote=, with metathesis of the first syllable as seen in the word’s descendants<ref>te Velde, Herman (1999) “Bastet” in ''{{w|Dictionary of Deities and Demons in the Bible}}'', second edition, page 165</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E|bꜣs_tt}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|bꜣk|t1=to work, to render service, to pay taxes|-w}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=baʀˈkuw|m=baʀˈkuw|ml=bəˈkuʔ|l=bəˈkøʔ}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|bꜣk|t1=servant|nj|alt2=n(j)|t2=of|rn|t3=name|.f|t4=his}}, thus ‘servant of his name’.

===Pronunciation===
