** {{desc|cop-fay|ⲛⲓⲃⲓ}}
** {{desc|cop-lyc|ⲛⲓⲙ}}
** {{desc|cop-oxy|ⲛⲓⲙ}}
** {{desc|cop-ppp|ⲛⲓⲃ}}
** {{desc|cop-sah|ⲛⲓⲙ}}

===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|egy|lord}}
Ehret attempts to derive this term from a {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*ruub-||to send}}; as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking, and in this case the derivation is not particularly plausible.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈniːbuw|m=ˈniːbuw|ml=neːbə|l=neːβ
|mnote=, in some circumstances reduced early to {{IPA|egy|/nib/}} {{q|by dialect? contingent on phonological context?}}<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|36, 55}}</ref>
|mlnote=&#x20;{{q|Lower Egypt?}}, {{IPA|egy|/neb/}} {{q|Upper Egypt?}}
|lnote=&#x20;{{q|Lower Egypt?}}, {{IPA|egy|/nep/}} {{q|Upper Egypt?}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{af|egy|m-|pos1=noun-forming prefix|alt2=*bw(j)|t2=abomination}} with regular dissimilation of {{m|egy|m-}} to {{m|egy|n-}} before a labial; for the stem, compare {{m|egy|bwt||abomination}}, {{m|egy|bwj||to abominate}}.<ref>Gundacker, Roman (2011) “On the Etymology of the Egyptian Crown Name ''mrsw.t''*: An “Irregular” Subgroup of ''m''-Prefix Formations” in ''Lingua Aegyptia'', volume 19, page 44</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|egy|/ˈnaβi/|a=Late Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 4===

====Romanization====
{{head|egy|romanization}}

