etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|nb|t1=lord|ꜣbḏw|t2=Abydos}} in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘lord of Abydos’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˌnibuwʀaˈbaːcʼaw/|a=Old Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E|nb-ꜣbḏ_w}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|nbw|t1=golden|kꜣ|alt2=kꜣw|t2=[[ka]]s|rꜥ|t3=[[Ra]]}}, thus ‘golden (one) of the [[ka]]s of [[Ra]]’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˈnaːbiw ˈkuʀwaw ˈɾiːʕuw/|a=Middle Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E|ang=nwb-kꜣw-rꜥ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nb-mꜣꜥt|t1=possessor of righteousness/truth|snfr|t2=to make pleasing, fine, beautiful|wj|t3=me}}, thus ‘the possessor of righteousness has made me fine’.{{attention|egy|What’s the verb form? Is this stative? I don’t know enough about Old Egyptian TAM marking to say}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|nb-mꜣꜥt-s_nfr-wj}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{compound|egy|nb|gloss1=lord|tꜣwj|gloss2=the Two Lands}} in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Lord of the Two Lands’, i.e. Upper and Lower Egypt.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˌnibuwˈtaʀwaj/|a=Old Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Nephthys}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|nbt|gloss1=lady|ḥwt|gloss2=enclosure}} in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Lady of the Enclosure’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2016|January|13}}

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*fir-||to be good}} with an additional ''*nV-'' prefix.<ref>{{R:HSED|*fir-}}</ref> Compare also {{cog|thv|ⵉⴼⵔⴰⵔ|tr=ifrar|t=best}} and {{cog|ssy|feer||good}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|q=adjective|o=ˈnaːfiɾ|m=ˈnaːfiʔ|ml=ˈnaːfa|l=ˈnoːfə
|onote=<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|39, 56}}</ref>
|mlnote=; compare the [[Akkadian]] transcription {{m|akk|𒈾𒀀𒉺|tr=na-a-pa}}<ref name=Loprieno/>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|nfr|t1=not even|ꜣ|pos2=intensifying particle}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Phrase===
{{head|egy|phrase|head=<hiero>nfr-f:r-A</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|with following imperfective emphatic form}} {{ng|forms a negative counterpart of the subjunctive in main or dependent clauses}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|414}}

===Etymology 2===
{{number box|egy|0}}
Related to {{m|egy|nfr||not even, not at all}}.

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nfrw <t:beauty>|ptḥ <t:Ptah>}} in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the beauty of Ptah’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|nfr_w-ptḥ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|nfrw|gloss1=beauty|rꜥ|gloss2=Ra}} in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘The Beauty of Ra’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|nfr_w-rꜥ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|nfr|gloss1=not even|n}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Phrase===
{{head|egy|phrase|head=<hiero>nfr-f:r:n</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|chiefly|Old Egyptian|with a following imperfective emphatic form}} [[not]], [[that]] [[not]]
# {{lb|egy|Middle Egyptian|with a following imperfective emphatic form}} {{ng|used to negate the [[protasis]] of conditional sentences}}

====Usage notes====
In Middle Egyptian, the use of the verb {{m|egy|tm||to not do, not be}} largely supplanted the construction with {{m|egy||nfr n}} except in protases.

====Alternative forms====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>nfr-f:r:D35</hiero>|read1=nfr nj}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|195, 414}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nfr <t:beautiful (one)>|nfrw <pos:plural of {{m|egy|nfr}}><t:beautiful ones>|jtn <t:Aten>}} in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘beautiful one of the beautiful ones of Aten’, i.e. ‘most beautiful one of Aten’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|ml=nafnafɾəˈjaːtəj|mlnote=<ref>{{cite-journal|first=Gerhard|last=Fecht|title=Amarra-Probleme (1–2)|journal=Zeitschrift für Ägyptische Sprache und Altertumskunde|volume=85|issue=1|year=1960|doi=10.1524/zaes.1960.85.1.99}}</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E|nfr-nfr_w-jtn}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nfr <t:beautiful (one)>|nfrw <pos:plural of {{m|egy|nfr}}><t:beautiful ones>|rꜥ <t:Ra>}} in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘beautiful one of the beautiful ones of Ra’, i.e. ‘most beautiful one of Ra’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|nfr-nfr_w-rꜥ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|nfr|t1=beautiful (one)|ḥtp|t2=to be content|.w|alt3=(.w)|pos3=masculine singular stative ending}}, thus literally ‘the beautiful one is content’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|ml=nafˈħatpə|l=nəfˈħatp(ə)}}
{{egy-IPA-E|nfr-ḥtp|nfr-ḥotp}}

==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Neferkare}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nfr <t:pleasing, fine, good>|kꜣ <t:[[ka]]>|rꜥ <t:Ra>}}, thus literally ‘the fine (one) of the [[ka]] of Ra’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nfr <t:pleasing, fine, good>|kꜣ <t:[[ka]]>|zkr <t:Sokar>}}, thus literally ‘the fine (one) of the [[ka]] of Sokar’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|nfrt|gloss1=beautiful (one)|jꜣbt|gloss2=east}} in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘the beautiful (one) of the east’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˌnafɾə ˈjoːβə/|a=Late Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|nfrt|t1=beautiful (one)|jrj|t2=with respect to it, thereto}}, thus roughly ‘the most beautiful (one) among them’. The {{m|egy||t}} and following {{m|egy||j}} in this name are often transposed for aesthetic reasons.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|ml=nafˈteːɾə|mlnote=, transcribed in cuneiform as {{m|akk|𒈾𒀊𒋼𒊏|tr=na-ap-te-ra|sc=Xsux}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

