Possibly from an earlier {{m|egy|nrt||Neith}}, a feminine form of {{m|egy|nrw||terror, fear, terrible one}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Formed from {{af|egy|nt|-j <pos:''[[nisba]]'' ending>}}, where {{m|egy|nt}} is a nominalized use of the feminine form of the genitival adjective {{m|egy|nj|n(j)}}. The noun {{m|egy||ntj}} is in turn simply a nominalized use of the adjective {{m|egy||ntj}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=niˈtij|m=niˈtij|ml=nətə|l=nət}}
{{egy-IPA-E|ntj|entj}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|nṯrj <t:divine (one)>|kꜣ <t:[[ka]]>|rꜥ <t:sun, [[Ra]]>}} in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘Divine One of the Ka of Ra’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/nucˈɾij kuʀ ˈɾiːʕuw/|a=Old Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E|nṯr_j-kꜣ-rꜥ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|nṯr|t1=god|ꜥꜣj|alt2=ꜥꜣ|t2=great, elder}}, thus (conventionally) ‘the great god’ or (more accurately) ‘the elder god’.<ref>For the latter translation see Berlev, Oleg (2003) “Два Царя — Два Солнца: К мировоззрению древних египтян” and the translated version “Two kings – Two Suns: On the Worldview of the Ancient Egyptians” in ''Discovering Egypt from the Neva: the Egyptological Legacy of Oleg D. Berlev'', edited by Quirke, Stepehen, Berlin: Achet-Verlag, pages 1–35</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=nacaɾˈʕaʀ|m=nataʔˈʕaʀ|ml=nətəˈʕaʔ|l=nətəˈʕaʔ}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Perhaps from a form such as {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*nx̣m}}. Cognate with {{cog|sem-pro|*nʿm-}}.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|32}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|q=verb|o=ˈnaːcʼam|m=ˈnaːtʼam|ml=ˈnaːtʼəm|l=ˈnoːtʼəm}}
