etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḥtp|t1=offering|ḏj|pos2=perfective relative form of {{m|egy|rḏj}}}}, thus ‘an offering that … gives’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Phrase===
{{head|egy|phrase|head=<hiero>Htp-di</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[conventionally]] used in the [[offering formula]] to introduce the name of a [[god]] whose [[local]] [[funerary]] [[establishment]] provides [[funerary]] [[gift]](s) for the [[deceased]]}}
## {{ng|as the introduction to the offering formula as a whole}} {{defdate|Pyramid Texts and Old Kingdom}}
## {{ng|following the term {{m|egy|ḥtp ḏj nswt}} at the start of the offering formula}} {{defdate|Pyramid Texts to Middle Kingdom and rarely in the 18th and 26th Dynasties}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Wb|3|186, 188.1}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|ḥtp <t:offering>|ḏj <pos:perfective relative form of {{m|egy|rḏj}}>|nswt <t:king>}}, thus ‘an offering that the king gives’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 2===
Nominalized use of the feminine form of {{m|egy|ḥḏ||white}}. Nisba suffix present, as shown by the Demotic form ''ḥṱy.t''.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ħucʼjat|m=ħutʼjaʔ|ml=ħutʼja|l=ħøtʼjə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḥḏ|t1=be(come) white|tꜣ|t2=land}}, literally ‘the land becomes white’, with {{m|egy|ḥḏ}} as a non-attributive relative (nominal/emphatic) form, effectively making the clause adverbial in meaning.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Phrase===
{{head|egy|phrase|head=<hiero>HD-D&ra-tA:N23*Z1</hiero>}}

# [[at]] [[dawn]]

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|385–386}}

==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Khafra}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|ḫꜥj <alt:ḫꜥ><t:to appear>|.f <t:he>|rꜥ <t:sun, Ra>}}, thus meaning ‘he appears as Ra’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia|Khufu}}

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḫwj|alt1=ḫw|t1=shall protect|pos1=subjunctive mood of {{m|egy|ḫwj||to protect}}|.f|t2=he|wj|t3=me}}, thus literally ’He shall protect me’; the longer version of the name, {{m|egy|ẖnmw-ḫw.f-wj}}, reveals that ‘he’ is the god [[Khnum]].

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=χawˈjafwij|m=χawˈjafwij|ml=χəˈwafwə|l=χəˈwaf(w)|ref=<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|82}}</ref><ref>Gundacker, Roman (2015) “The Chronology of the Third and Fourth Dynasties according to Manetho’s Aegyptiaca” in ''Towards a New History for the Egyptian Old Kingdom'', page 114–115, provides the final vowel but disagrees with Loprieno in some details of the word’s subsequent development: where Loprieno considers the semivowels preceding the tonic {{IPAfont|/a/}} to have ultimately reduced to glottal stops, Gundacker posits that {{IPAfont|/j/}} assimilated to the preceding {{IPAfont|/w/}}, which was preserved.</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E|ḫw.f-wj|ḫwfw}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḫw.n|pos1=perfect relative form of {{m|egy|ḫwj}}|jnpw|t2=Anubis}}, thus literally ‘(he) whom Anubis has protected’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈχaːpaɾ|m=ˈχaːpaʔ|ml=ˈχaːpa|l=ˈχoːpə|ref=<ref>Loprieno, Antonio (2001) “From Ancient Egyptian to Coptic” in Haspelmath, Martin et al. (eds.), ''Language Typology and Language Universals''.</ref>}}
