<references/>

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ẖrj|t1=lying under, being the place of|nṯr|t2=god}}, thus literally ‘that which is under the god’ or ’place where the god is’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=çuˈɾij ˈnaːcaɾ|m=çuˈɾij ˈnaːtaʔ|ml=çəˈɾej ˈnaːta|l=çəˈɾej ˈnoːtə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ẖrj|t1=lying under|tp|t2=head|nswt|t3=king}}, thus literally ‘(one) under the head of the king’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 2===
Speculatively, Ehret hypothesizes an origin in {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*ji|pos=one, someone, somebody}}; as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking. Compare {{cog|he|זֶה}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=zuʀ|m=suʀ|ml=suʔ|l=søʔ
|onote=&#x20;{{q|singular}}; {{IPA|egy|/ˈzuʀwaw/}} {{q|plural}}
|mnote=&#x20;{{q|singular}}; {{IPA|egy|/ˈsuʀwaw/}} {{q|plural}}
|mlnote=&#x20;{{q|singular}}; {{IPA|egy|/ˈsuːwə/}} {{q|plural}}
|lnote=&#x20;{{q|singular}}; {{IPA|egy|/seːw/}} {{q|plural}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E|z|zꜣ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|zꜣ <t:son>|jmn <t:[[Amun]]>}} in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘son of [[Amun]]’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 3===
Most likely derived from a form of the verb {{m|egy||zꜣw|to guard, to protect}}, perhaps via an earlier form {{m|egy||*zꜣjw}}, for which see the reconstructed pronunciation.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈzuʀjVw|m=ˈsuʀjVw|ml=ˈsuʔjə|l=ˈsøʔjə
|onote=<ref>{{R:egy:Osing|376, 405, 425}}</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Possibly from a form such as {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*ǯiʔib-||beast of prey}}. Compare {{cog|he|זאב|זְאֵב|tr=zǝʾēḇ}} and {{cog|ar|ذِئْب}} from {{cog|sem-pro|*ḏiʔb-||wolf}}, as well as Chadic ''ǯib-'' “civet”.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈziːʀib|m=ˈsiːʀib|ml=ˈsiːʔəb|l=ˈsiːʔəβ}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|zꜣ|gloss1=son|nht|gloss2=sycamore}} in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘son of sycamore’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|m=siːʀ ˈnaːhaʔ|ml=siːʔ ˈnaːha|l=siːʔ ˈnoːhə}}
* {{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|zꜣ <t:son>|nswt <t:king>}} in a direct genitive construction. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|zꜣ|sbk|gloss1=son|gloss2=[[Sobek]]}} in a direct genitive construction, literally ‘son of Sobek’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 2===
{{af|egy|zꜣ|t1=son|tꜣ|t2=earth}} in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘son of the earth’.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈziʀtaʀ|m=ˈsiʀtaʀ|ml=ˈsiːtə|l=siːt
|onote=<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|57}}</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|zꜣt <t:daughter>|nswt <t:king>}} in a direct genitive construction. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

zwt.references:
* {{R:egy:Wb|3|426.12–426.17}}
* {{R:egy:Faulkner|218}}
* {{R:egy:LÄ|6|1210|passage=Äg. ''zwt'' sollte nicht mit „W[eizen]“ übersetzt werden; es dürfte sich um eine Emmer-Varietät handeln.}}

etymologies.etymology_text:
===Etymology 1===
Related to {{m|egy|zf||to slaughter, cut up}}, {{m|egy|zfṯ||to slaughter}}. Also compare {{cog|ar|سيف|tr=sayf}}, {{cog|am|ሰይፍ}}, {{cog|grc|ξίφος}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈzuːfat|m=ˈsuːfaʔ|ml=ˈsuːfa|l=ˈseːfə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Related to {{m|egy|zf||to slaughter, cut up}}, {{m|egy|zft||sword, knife}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|q=verb|o=ˈzaːfac|m=ˈsaːfaʔ|ml=ˈsaːfa|l=ˈsoːfə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|znj|t1=to surpass|mnt|t2=suffering}}, with {{m|egy|znj}} in the infinitive and the two words in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘surpassing of suffering’.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|ml=ˌsənəˈmaːna|l=ˌsənəˈmoːnə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Egyptian_-_Stone_Weight_of_Sesostris_I_-_Walters_4131.jpg|thumb|Inscription in which an alternative form of the name '''z-n-wsrt''' can be read.]]

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|z <t:man>|nj <alt:n(j)><t:of>|wsrt <t:[[Wosret]]>}}, thus ‘man of Wosret’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=zuʀ nij ˈwasɾit|m=suʀ nij ˈwasɾiʔ|ml=suʔ nə ˈwasɾa|l=søʔ nə ˈwasɾə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*ʒin-}} with an uncertain suffix -''f'', according to Orel and Stolbova’s very tentative reconstruction.<ref>{{R:HSED|section=2626|page=546|*ʒin-}}</ref> If so, perhaps cognate with West Chadic *''ʒin-'' (“blood”), whence {{cog|ha|jini}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|q=noun|o=zVˈnaf|m=sVˈnaf|ml=səˈnaf|l=səˈnaf|V=1}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{af|egy|zḥ <t:booth, hall, embalmer’s workshop>|nṯr <t:god>}} in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘booth of the god’, ‘divine booth’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|zt|t1=woman|ḥmt|t2=woman}}. This compound arose because the feminine ending {{m|egy|-t}} in {{m|egy|zt}} eventually became silent, making {{m|egy|zt}} homophonous with {{m|egy|z||man}}, so that it became necessary to add {{m|egy|ḥmt}} to clarify.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˌzutˈħiːmat|m=ˌsuʔˈħiːmaʔ|ml=səˈħiːma|l=səˈħiːmə}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*s-|pos=causative prefix}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Prefix===
{{egy-prefix|head=<hiero>s</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Used to form a causative verb from a non-causative verb.}}

====Alternative forms====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>z</hiero>|read1=s}}

====Derived terms====
{{prefixsee|egy}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|p=157}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|egy|sem}}, ultimately from {{der|egy|sem-pro|*ṯalg-}}; compare {{cog|he|שֶׁלֶג|tr=šéleḡ}}, {{cog|akk|𒊭𒆷𒄖|tr=šalgu}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/salɡuː/|a=Middle Egyptian|ref={{R:egy:Hoch 1994|264}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2019|March|16}}

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|s-|pos1=causative prefix|jꜣṯ|gloss2=to be injured}}; despite the derivation from {{m|egy|jꜣṯ}}, it appears the expected form {{m|egy|*sjꜣṯ}} is unattested.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E|s_jꜣt}}

==Egyptian==
{{number box|egy|6}}

===Etymology===
Compare {{cog|sem-pro|*šidṯ-|*šidṯum}}, {{cog|tzm|ⵙⴹⵉⵙ}}, {{cog|ha|shidà}}, and {{cog|awn|ሴዛ|tr=sedza|t=four}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=saɾˈsaw|m=sajˈsaw|ml=ʔəsˈsaw|l=ʔəsˈsaw|ref=<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|71}}</ref><ref>{{R:Blažek:1999|pages=28–56}}</ref>
|onote=; the first vowel has alternatively been reconstructed as {{IPAfont|/i/}}
|mlnote=, with metathesis; compare the Akkadian transcription in {{cog|akk-mbb|𒄑𒊭𒌑|tr=⸢is⸣-ša-u<sub>2</sub>|ts=issau}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 2===

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=saˈbiʀ|m=saˈbiʀ|ml=səˈbeʔ|l=səˈβeʔ
|onote=<ref>{{R:cop:Vycichl|184}}</ref>
|mlnote=; compare the Akkadian transcription in {{cog|akk-mbb|𒁍𒊻𒁉𒌋|tr=pu-us-bi-u<sub>2</sub>|ts=pusbiu}}, with definite article {{m|egy|pꜣ}} attached.<ref>{{cite-web|title=EA 368|titleurl=http://cdli.ucla.edu/P270862|site=Cuneiform Digital Library Initiative: CDLI|url=http://cdli.ucla.edu|year=2021}}</ref>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˈsaptʼacʼ|m=ˈsaptʼaʔ|ml=ˈsaptʼa|l=ˈsaptʼə
|onote=<ref>Allen, James (2013) ''The Ancient Egyptian Language: An Historical Study'', Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, {{ISBN|9781107664678}}, page 28</ref>
|mnote=, with unexpected sound change {{IPAfont|/t’/}} > {{IPAfont|/t/}} (> {{IPAfont|/ʔ/}})}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

