etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḏsr|t1=sacred|kꜣ|t2=[[ka]]|rꜥ|t3=Ra}}, thus literally ‘Sacred (One) of the [[ka|Ka]] of [[Ra]]’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|egy|/ˈtʼaːsaʔ kuʔ ˈɾiːʕəʔ/|a=Medio-Late Egyptian}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

ḏt.references:
* {{R:egy:Allen|95, 109–110, 173, 187, 279}}
* {{R:egy:Wb}}
* Petrie, Flinders (1900) ''The royal tombs of the first dynasty'', part I, plate XVII, 125-127
* {{R:egy:Beckerath|171}}

{{C|egy|Cosmology|Law|Plants|Reptiles|Society}}

etymologies.etymology_text:
===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=cʼaːtʼ|m=t͡ʃʼaːʔ|ml=t͡ʃʼaːʔ|l=t͡ʃʼoːʔ
|onote=&#x20;{{q|infinitive}}; {{IPA|egy|/ˈcʼitʼnu/}} {{q|perfect relative form}}<ref>{{R:egy:Loprieno|37, 53}}</ref>
|mnote=&#x20;{{q|infinitive}}, with unexpected sound change {{IPAfont|/tʼ/}} → {{IPAfont|/t/}} (→ {{IPAfont|/ʔ/}})
|mlnote=&#x20;{{q|infinitive}}
|lnote=&#x20;{{q|infinitive}}}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{com+|egy|ḏd|t1=to speak|pꜣ|t2=the|nṯr|t3=god|jw|pos4=''proclitic particle''|.f|pos5=''masculine suffix pronoun''|ꜥnḫ|t6=to live}}, thus producing "the god speaks, and he lives".<ref>Steindorff, Georg; ''Der Name Josephs Saphenat–Paʽneach''; Zeitschrift für Ägyptische Sprache und Altertumskunde, Vol. XXVII. 42, 1889; {{ISSN|2196-713X}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|l=t͡ʃʼapəˈnaːtə ʔəfʕəˈnaχ}}
{{egy-IPA-E|ḏd-pꜣ-nṯr-jw.f-ꜥnḫ}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḏd|t1=to say|pw|t2=this (is)}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Phrase===
{{head|egy|phrase|head=<hiero>D:d-p-w</hiero>}}

# {{ng|introduces [[explanatory]] [[gloss]]es to a text}}; [[that is to say]] …; it [[mean]]s …

===References===
* {{R:egy:TLA|ḏd-pw|186020}}
* {{R:egy:Wb|5|625.1–625.2}}
* {{R:egy:Faulkner|325}}

==Egyptian==
{{was fwotd|egy|2013|June|26}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|egy|ḏd|mdw|gloss1=to say|gloss2=speech, words}}, with {{m|egy|ḏd}} in the infinitive.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=ˌcʼaːtʼ maˈtʼuww|m=ˌt͡ʃʼaːʔ maˈtʼuww|ml=t͡ʃʼəməˈtʼuww|l=t͡ʃʼəməˈtʼøww}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
{{af|egy|ḏḏt|pos1=feminine imperfective relative form of {{m|egy|rḏj}}|jmn|t2=[[Amon]]}}, thus literally ‘(she) whom Amon gives’. {{egy-ht}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Probably from {{m|egy|jn}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Suffix===
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>i-n</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[consecutive]] form of all verbs.}}

====Alternative forms====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>i-N</hiero>|read1=.jn}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|307}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From Proto-Egyptian *''-su'', the back vowel /u/ led to labialization of /s/.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|64}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=f|m=f|ml=f|l=f|ref=<ref name=Loprieno/>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 1===
Loprieno hypothesizes development from an earlier form {{m|egy||*-ina}}.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|64}}</ref> In any case, perhaps ultimately from a form such as {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*ˀan-||pos=first-person pronoun (plural exclusive?)}}; compare {{cog|irk|-ni}}, {{cog|ar|ـنَا}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=in|m=in|ml=en|l=en|onote=<ref name=Loprieno/>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Etymology 2===
Most likely grammaticalized from the preposition {{m|egy|n||to, for}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-E}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>n</hiero>}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Forms the [[perfect]] of all verbs.}}
# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|Middle Egyptian}} {{ng|Forms the [[perfect]] relative form of all verbs.}}
# {{lb|egy|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally marks the [[preterite]] of all verbs.}}
# {{lb|egy|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Optionally marks the [[perfective]] [[active]] relative form of all verbs.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>N</hiero>|read1=.n}}

.n.references:
* {{R:egy:Allen|50, 227}}
* {{R:egy:Junge|52, 66}}
* Werning, Daniel A. (2008) “Aspect vs. Relative Tense, and the Typological Classification of the Ancient Egyptian ''sḏm.n⸗f''” in ''Lingua Aegyptia'' 16, pages 261–292
<references/>

etymologies.etymology_text:
===Etymology 2===
{{af|egy|.n|pos1=perfect suffix|-j|pos2=adverbializing suffix}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-E|.n_j}}

====Suffix====
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>n:y</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Sometimes used to form the [[perfect]] of verbs when the subject is omitted.}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From {{m|egy|ḫr||must, has to}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Suffix===
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>x:r</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[obligative]] form of all verbs.}}

====Alternative forms====
{{egy-hieroforms
|<hiero>x:r:Y1</hiero>|read1=ḫr|note1=rare}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|310, 312}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*-ka}} or {{m|afa-pro|*-ku}}. Cognate with {{cog|ar|كَ|ـكَ}}, {{cog|irk|-ku}} and {{cog|ha|kai}}.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|31, 64}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-IPA-R|o=k|m=k|ml=k|l=k|ref=<ref name=Loprieno/>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
Probably from {{m|egy|kꜣ||so, then, as a result}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-E}}

===Suffix===
{{egy-suffix|head=<hiero>k-A</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Forms the [[potentialis]] form of all verbs.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|312}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From Proto-Egyptian *''-kʲi'', from {{inh|egy|afa-pro|*-ki|pos=second-person possessive suffix}}.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|31, 64}}</ref> Compare {{cog|irk|-ki}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=c|m=ʔ|ml=(ʔ)|l=(ʔ)
|onote=<ref name=Loprieno />
|mnote=, from earlier Middle Egyptian {{IPA|egy|/t/}}
|ml2={{IPA|egy|/ʔ/}} in a stressed syllable; otherwise silent
|l2={{IPA|egy|/ʔ/}} in a stressed syllable; otherwise silent}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Etymology===
From Proto-Egyptian *''-kina'', the front vowel /i/ led to a palatalization of /k/.<ref name=Loprieno>{{R:egy:Loprieno|64}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-IPA-R|o=cin|m=tin|ml=ten|l=ten|onote=<ref name=Loprieno/>}}
{{egy-IPA-E}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a seated man with both arms flexed, the rearward arm overlapping the body, essentially portraying a man in a generic pose to draw little attention to any particular action or quality other than manhood. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|人|sc=Hani}}. {{egy-hiero-color-men}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A1</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|z||man}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian}} {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|rmṯ||human, person}}.}}<ref name=Uljas>Uljas, Sami (2022) “The Destruction of ‘Mankind’” in ''Zeitschrift für Ägyptische Sprache und Altertumskunde'', volume 149, issue 2, pages 274–280</ref>
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|.j|pos=first person suffix pronoun}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|Late Egyptian}} {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|wj|pos=first person dependent pronoun}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for the relations, occupations, and personal names of men.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for the first person, as in {{m|egy|jnk}}, {{m|egy|.kw}}, {{m|egy|wj}}.}}
# {{ng|Part of the combination <hiero>A1:Z2</hiero>, a logogram for {{m|egy|rḥw||comrades}} in certain personal names.}}
# {{ng|Part of the combination <hiero>A1*B1:Z2</hiero>, a [[determinative]] for groups of people, as in {{m|egy|rmṯ|rmṯw|people}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|442}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|15}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Junge|77–78}}
<references/>

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a man sitting on his heel. {{egy-hiero-color-men}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A3</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḥmsj||to sit}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>In hieratic script, this glyph is replaced by [[𓀔]] (<hiero>A17</hiero>) or [[𓀕]] (<hiero>A17A</hiero>), or in one instance even [[𓁓]] (<hiero>B4</hiero>).</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|442}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a man with his arms raised. {{egy-hiero-color-men}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A4</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] meaning "supplicate", such as in {{m|egy|dwꜣ||praise}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] meaning "hide", such as in {{m|egy|sdgꜣ||be hidden}}, and {{m|egy|jmn||hide}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph's usage is closely aligned with the usage of [[𓀢]] (<hiero>A30</hiero>). Compare also [[𓀄]] (<hiero>A5</hiero>), which was similar in early depictions (before c. the 18th Dynasty, when that figure started to be depicted crouching behind a wall).</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|442}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a man crouching with his arms raised, hiding. In later depictions (since partway through the 18th Dynasty), the man is hiding behind a wall; in earlier depictions the wall is not present, and the glyph was visually similar to {{m|egy|𓀃}} ({{egy-h|A4}}) but for the different posture. {{egy-hiero-color-men}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|A5}}|tr=-}}{{attention|egy|Added headword template. Don't know if this needs anything else.}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for {{m|egy|jmn||hide}}.}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a fallen man with blood streaming from his head. {{egy-hiero-color-men}} The blood is red. Some scribes reinterpreted or depicted the blood as an axe, with a red (wood) handle and white (simple stone) axehead.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A14</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for death, as in {{m|egy|mwt||to die}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for enemies, as in {{m|egy|ḫftj||enemy}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|443}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a man with his hands raised on either side. {{egy-hiero-color-men}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A28</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|qꜣj||to be(come) high}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|jꜣs||to be(come) bald}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to joy or happiness.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to mourning.}}

====Descendants====
* Proto-Sinaitic: [[Image:Proto-semiticE-01.svg|20px]]
** Proto-Canaanite: [[Image:Protohe.svg|20px]]
*** {{desctree|phn|𐤄}}
** {{desc|sem-srb|𐩠}}
*** {{desc|gez|ሀ}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an upright mummy.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A53</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for "mummy", such as in {{m|egy|wj||mummy}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for "statue" or "likeness", such as in {{m|egy|twt||statue}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for "form" or "shape", such as in {{m|egy|qj||form}} and {{m|egy|ḫprw||forms, stages of growth}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|447}}

==Egyptian==
{{multiple images|perrow = 2|total_width=180
| image1 = Abydos-Bold-hieroglyph-B1-rtl-square.png  |width1=90|height1=90
| image2 = Hieroglyph B1 color Nakht.png  |width2=90|height2=90
| image3 = Hieratic B1 5D.png  |width3=90|height3=90
| image4 = Hieratic B1 20D.png  |width4=90|height4=90
| footer = Top left: hieroglyph B1 (stylized); right: in color, 18th Dynasty. <br/>Bottom left: hieratic, 5th Dynasty; right: 20th Dynasty.
}}

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a seated woman wrapped tightly in a dress or cloak and wearing a long wig, essentially portraying a woman in a generic pose to draw little attention to any particular action or quality other than womanhood. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|女|sc=Hani}}. Old Kingdom inscriptions often show this hieroglyph as slightly smaller than the seated man, <hiero>A1</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓀀}}), but in later times they are of equal size.</div>

Conventionally, the woman's skin is colored yellow, distinguishing her from men (who have red skin)<!--; rare exceptions exist where a powerful woman's skin is colored red-->. The hair is conventionally black; the robe is conventionally either white or (starting in the Third Intermediate Period) sometimes dark red.[[Category:Polychrome Egyptian hieroglyphs]]<!--because multiple colors-->[[Category:Yellow Egyptian hieroglyphs]]<!--because the defining part is yellow-->

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>B1</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|.j|pos=first-person suffix pronoun}} when the speaker is female.}} {{defdate|since the 19th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for the relations, occupations, and names of women and words associated with the female sphere in general.}}
# {{ng|Part of the combination <hiero>A1*B1:Z2</hiero>, a [[determinative]] for groups of people, as in {{m|egy|rmṯ|rmṯw|people}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a seated pregnant woman with possibly a ritual knot or tie in her hair that may symbolise tightness that is to be released during labor.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>B2</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for pregnancy, as in {{m|egy|jwr||to conceive}}, {{m|egy|bkꜣ||to be(come) pregnant}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a woman giving birth, in the posture that would typically be adopted by Egyptian women in such a scenario (crouching over birth bricks, though the bricks themselves are not depicted).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>B3</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|msj||to give birth}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for giving birth, as in {{m|egy|msj||to give birth}}, {{m|egy|pꜥpꜥ||to give birth}}, etc.}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|egy|<hiero>B4</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓁓}})}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a woman giving birth, with the depiction of the child’s emerging head and arms seen in {{m|egy|𓁒}} (<hiero>B3</hiero>) symbolically replaced by [[fennec]] skins from the hieroglyph {{m|egy|𓄟}} (<hiero>ms</hiero>), itself used in various words relating to birth and children. In old examples the woman and the skins are found almost or completely separated.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>B4</hiero>}}

# {{alt form|egy|𓁒}} (<hiero>B3</hiero>)
# {{lb|egy|rare|in a single hieratic manuscript}} {{alt form|egy|𓀂}} (<hiero>A3</hiero>)

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a woman breastfeeding a child in the usual posture (seated on the ground). Simplified version: {{m|egy|𓁕}} ({{egy-h|B104}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|B5}}}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for nursing or breastfeeding, as in {{m|egy|mnꜥ||to nurse, to suckle}}, {{m|egy|mnꜥt||wet nurse}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a woman seated on a chair holding a child in her lap.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>B6</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|rnn||to bring up, to nurse}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a seated queen wearing a diadem and carrying a flower.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>B7</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in names of queens.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a god with sun and uraeus on its head.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C1</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|rꜥ||the sun-god}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a god with the head of a falcon bearing sun on head <hiero>G9</hiero> and holding <hiero>S34</hiero>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C2</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|rꜥ||the sun-god}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a god with the head of an [[ibis]], {{l|egy|𓅝}} (<hiero>G26</hiero>) or {{l|egy|𓅞}} (<hiero>G26A</hiero>).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C3</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḏḥwtj||Thoth}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a god with the head of a ram, {{l|egy|𓃝}} (<hiero>E10</hiero>).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C4</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ẖnmw||Chnum}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|448}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
The same as {{l|egy|𓁠}} (<hiero>C4</hiero>), but holding {{l|egy|𓋹}} (<hiero>S34</hiero>).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C5</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ẖnmw||Chnum}} (same as <hiero>C4</hiero>)}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a god with the head of a dog {{l|egy|𓃢}} (<hiero>E15</hiero>).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C6</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|jnpw||Anubis}} as well as {{m|egy|wp-wꜣwt||Wepwawet}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a god with the head of a Seth-animal {{l|egy|𓃩}} (<hiero>E20</hiero>).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C7</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] in {{m|egy|stẖ||Set}}}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing ithyphallic god with feathers, uplifted arm, and flagellum <hiero>S45</hiero>.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C8</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|mnw||Min}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a goddess with sun and horns.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C9</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḥwt-ḥr||Hathor}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Goddess with feather on head

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>C10</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|mꜣꜥt||Maat, the goddess of truth}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a male head wearing a wig, in profile, typically bearded. The style of the wig varies by time period; the earlier style, from the Old Kingdom, is longer. Sometimes there is no beard. The skin is conventionally colored red; the hair is black; in detailed examples, the sclera is white and the eye and other facial features are outlined in black.[[Category:Polychrome Egyptian hieroglyphs]]<!--because multiple colors-->[[Category:Red Egyptian hieroglyphs]]<!--because the main part is red-->

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>tp</hiero>|tr=tp}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||tp}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|tp||head}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ḏꜣḏꜣ||head}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for a number of words relating to the head or actions implying movement of the head.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|gwꜣ||to pull together}}.}}

====Usage notes====
The reading of this sign (and the word {{m|egy|tp||head}} and its derivatives) has recently been contested. Werning and others have suggested that the traditional reading of {{m|egy||tp}} is wrong, with the New Kingdom reading more likely being {{m|egy|dp}}, and the original reading conceivably either {{m|egy|dp}} or {{m|egy|ḏp}}. However, other Egyptologists argue for the traditional reading, and indeed the bulk of the current evidence seems to point to the traditional value.<ref>Werning, Daniel A. (2004) “The Sound Values of the Signs Gardiner D1 (Head) and T8 (Dagger)” in ''Lingua Aegyptia'', volume 12, pages 183–204</ref><ref>Peust, Carsten (2006) “Nochmals zur Lesung der Kopf-Hieroglyphe” in ''Göttinger Miszellen'', volume 208, pages 7–8</ref><ref>Schweitzer, Simon D. (2011) “Zum Lautwert einiger Hieroglyphen” in ''Zeitschrift für Agyptische Sprache und Altertumskunde'', volume 138, pages 132–149</ref><ref>Werning, Daniel A. (2015) ''Einführung in die hieroglyphisch-ägyptische Schrift und Sprache'', pages 34–35:
*:“Die Einwände von (Schweitzer 2011) gegen die Lesung {{m|egy||dp}} sind nicht stichhaltig (was an anderer Stelle zu zeigen ist). Neben den unzweifelhaften Schreibungen von ‘Kopf’ als <hiero>d:p</hiero> im Amduat (Werning 2004: 196) ist noch folgender, kursivhieroglyphisch geschriebener Beleg hinzuzufügen: <hiero>Dsr:r-Y1-d:p-tp:Z1-A40</hiero> ‘Der ein „sakrales“ Haupt hat’ (Papyrus BM 9971).”</ref><ref>Roberson, Joshua Aaron (2018) “Tête-à-tête: Some Observations and Counter-Arguments Regarding a Contentious Phonological Value, ''dp'' or ''tp''” in ''Lingua Aegyptia'', volume 26, pages 185–202</ref>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|449–450}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)
<references/>

