etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a man’s face with a squared-off beard, one of the few hieroglyphs depicted in frontal view. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|面|sc=Hani}}, {{m|mul|𦣻|sc=Hani}}. The elements of the face tend to be somewhat flattened and exaggerated. Although the face is male, the skin is traditionally colored yellow, as if female, rather than the expected red; compare {{m|egy|𓂉}}, which is also often yellow. The hair is conventionally black; the beard is typically also black, but sometimes it is yellow as if it has been reinterpreted as the neck.<!--[[Category:Polychrome Egyptian hieroglyphs]] - because multiple colors?-->[[Category:Yellow Egyptian hieroglyphs]]<!--because the main part is yellow--> The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ḥr}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as the logogram for {{m|egy|ḥr||face}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>Hr</hiero>|tr=ḥr}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḥr}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḥr||face; upon}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|450}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|18}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)

𓁷.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|450}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|18}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the human eye. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|目|sc=Hani}}. The hieroglyph was given a somewhat elongated appearance with the iris partly covered by the upper lid. The outline of the eye was generally black; the sclera was white; the iris was often red (sometimes with a black pupil), but sometimes black. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||jr}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as the logogram for {{m|egy|jrt||eye}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>ir</hiero>|tr=jr}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy|jr}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jrt||eye}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to sight and the eye.}}
# {{ng|Part of the combination <hiero>U2:ir</hiero>, a [[biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||mꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|Part of the combination <hiero>ir:ir</hiero>, a [[logogram]] for {{m|egy|mꜣꜣ||to see}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|450}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|18}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)

[[Category:Polychrome Egyptian hieroglyphs]]

