etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the Eye of Horus or {{m|egy|wḏꜣt||lit=Intact One}} in the form of a human eye with [[:File:Lanner Falon close up bokeh (7913333524).jpg|the markings of a falcon’s head]]. The vertical stripe represents the dark spot found beneath the falcon’s eye, while the spiral stylizes the feather pattern behind this spot.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>wDAt</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|wḏꜣt||Eye of Horus}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|wḏꜣt||Eye of Horus}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|451}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
{{Egyptian eye fraction|the forward-facing part of the white of the eye}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D11</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Used as a numeral for {{frac|1|2}} in (mostly [[hieratic]]) measures of [[grain]].}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|106}}
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|451}}
* {{R:egy:Ritter 2002}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
{{Egyptian eye fraction|the pupil of the eye}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D12</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Used as a numeral for {{frac|1|4}} in (mostly [[hieratic]]) measures of [[grain]].}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḏfḏ||pupil (of the eye)}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph should not be confused with the similar hieroglyph N33 ({{m|egy|𓈒}}), existing in differently sized variants <hiero>N33B</hiero>, <hiero>N33C</hiero>, or <hiero>N33</hiero>, a determinative for grains and roundness.</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|106}}
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|451}}
* {{R:egy:Ritter 2002}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
{{Egyptian eye fraction|the rearward-facing part of the white of the eye}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D14</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Used as a numeral for {{frac|1|16}} in (mostly [[hieratic]]) measures of [[grain]].}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|106}}
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|451}}
* {{R:egy:Ritter 2002}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
{{Egyptian eye fraction|one of the markings of a falcon’s head found below the eye}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D15</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Used as a numeral for {{frac|1|32}} in (mostly [[hieratic]]) measures of [[grain]].}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|106}}
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|451}}
* {{R:egy:Ritter 2002}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
{{Egyptian eye fraction|one of the markings of a falcon’s head found below the eye}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D16</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Used as a numeral for {{frac|1|64}} in (mostly [[hieratic]]) measures of [[grain]].}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Allen|106}}
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|452}}
* {{R:egy:Ritter 2002}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the human mouth. In careful depictions, there is a significant narrowing toward the corners, so that the lips recurve, and the upper lip is more curved than the lower. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|口|sc=Hani}}. The phonetic value of {{m|egy||r}} is derived by the rebus principle from its use as the logogram for {{m|egy|r|r(ꜣ)|mouth}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>r</hiero>|tr=r}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||r}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|r|r(ꜣ)|mouth}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|452}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Paheri.jpg|right|thumb|An example of the breast hieroglyph from the tomb of Paheri, bottom of the second column from the left, in the word ''[[mnꜥt]]''.]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a breast. Sometimes depicted larger, <hiero>D27A</hiero> ({{l|egy|𓂒}}). {{egy-hiero-color|yellow}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D27</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|mnḏ||breast}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for<!-- {{m|egy|mnḏ||breast}} and--> {{m|egy|mnꜥt||wet nurse}}.}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing two extended arms, as if to embrace. The glyph was painted red, except for some instances where it had a white fill due to a yellow background, and a yellow fill due to a white background.<ref>David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)</ref>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|tr=kꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||kꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy||kꜣ|soul, spirit}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|453}}
<references/>

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the human forearm and elbow. In Old Kingdom depictions, the hand is flat in the vertical plane so that its full breadth is shown. Later forms show the palm slightly cupped upward instead, but all the fingers remain distinguished in detailed depictions through the New Kingdom. The most detailed examples of all tend to follow the Old Kingdom form. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} The phonetic value of {{m|egy||ꜥ}} is derived by the rebus principle from its use as the logogram for {{m|egy|ꜥ||arm}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>a</hiero>|tr=ꜥ}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ꜥ}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ꜥ||arm}}.}}
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂞}} (<hiero>D37</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂟}} (<hiero>D38</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂠}} (<hiero>D39</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂡}} (<hiero>D40</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂢}} (<hiero>D41</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂣}} (<hiero>D42</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂤}} (<hiero>D43</hiero>).
# {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓂥}} (<hiero>D44</hiero>).

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|454}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Hoch|50, 56}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Abydos KL 02-05 n13.jpg|right|thumb|The hand hieroglyph in the name Senedj.]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a human hand. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|手|sc=Hani}} and {{noncog|emy|-}} [[File:Maya Syllabary Ke 1.jpg|30px]] / [[File:Maya Syllabary Ke 2.jpg|30px]] ("hand"<!--; whence e.g. {{noncog|yua|k'ab}}-->).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>d</hiero>|tr=d}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||d}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ḏrt#Etymology 1|ḏrt|hand}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|455}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a human phallus. During the Old Kingdom, this glyph was used to represent the organ and the concepts it represented, while {{m|egy|𓂺}} <hiero>D53</hiero> was used to represent the actions the organ performed or the substances issued from it. During the Middle Kingdom, usage of these two signs differed and became less consistent. {{egy-hiero-color|red}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D52</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for "male", such as in {{m|egy|ꜥꜣ||ass}}, {{m|egy|ṯꜣy||male, man}}, and {{m|egy|kꜣ||bull}}.}}
# {{ng|[[phonetic|Phonetic]] {{m|egy|mt}}, such as in {{m|egy|mtwt||poison}} and {{m|egy|ḫmt||three}}}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|456}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a pair of forward-facing walking legs. From the Old Kingdom (Dynasties 3–6) onward this glyph would sometimes include a kilt. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} Sometimes the space between the legs was painted white.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D54</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jwj||to come}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|nmtt||stride}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for movement, as in {{m|egy|šmj||to go}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for lack of movement, as in {{m|egy|ꜣb||to stop}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|Old Kingdom|when elongated}} {{alt form|egy|𓏎}} (<hiero>ini</hiero>)

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|457}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|19}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a stylized row of three toes oriented leftward.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>D61</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy||sꜣḥ|toe}}.}}
# {{ng|[[phonetic|Phonetic]] {{m|egy|sꜣḥ}}}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|456}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
A depiction of an unidentified animal associated with [[Set]], the {{w|Set animal}}: a slender [[canid]] resembling a greyhound, [[Saluki]] or jackal, with a distinctive stiff tail (often forked in later depictions) which stands erect whether the animal is sitting, standing, or walking. Its erect ears are usually depicted as squarish or triangular, narrowest at the base and widest at the squarish(perhaps cropped) tops, and its long nose often has a slight downward curve. It may or may not have been a real animal. It is usually depicted at rest, either sitting like this, or lying down: {{l|egy|𓃫}} (<hiero>E21</hiero>).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>E20</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] in {{m|egy|stẖ||Set}}}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in e.g. {{m|egy|ẖnnw||turmoil}}.}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
A variant of {{l|egy|𓃩}} (<hiero>E20</hiero>) which is lying down.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>E21</hiero>}}

# {{altform|egy|𓃩}}
## {{ng|[[ideogram|Ideogram]] for {{m|egy|stẖ||Set}}}}
## {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in e.g. {{m|egy|nšnj||thunderstorm}}.}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Alexander_the_Great.jpg|right|thumb|representing l on a cartouche of Alexander the Great]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a recumbent lion. {{egy-hiero-color|yellow}} The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||rw}} derives by the rebus principle from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|rw||lion}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>rw</hiero>|tr=rw}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||rw}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|later}} {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||r}}, sometimes representing /l/.}}
# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||šnꜥ}}, through confusion with hieratic <hiero>U13</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓍁}}).}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|rw||lion}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|mꜣj||lion}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|460}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia}}

===Glyph origin===
A crouching depiction of the Egyptian desert hare ({{taxlink|Lepus capensis aegyptius|species}}), called {{m|egy|sẖꜥt}}. The use of the hare as a phonogram for {{m|egy||wn}} could derive from an ancient epithet of ‘runner’ for the hare. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|兔|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>E34</hiero>|tr=wn}}

# {{ng|Biliteral phonogram for {{m|egy||wn}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|wn||desert hare}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|461}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the head of an [[ox]]. Compare cuneiform {{m|mul|𒄞|sc=Xsux}} and the Chinese character [[牛]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>F1</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Form of {{m|egy|𓃒|<hiero>E1</hiero>}} used in the [[offering formula]] as a [[logogram]] for {{m|egy|kꜣ|kꜣw|oxen}}.}}

====Descendants====
{{top2}}
* Proto-Sinaitic: {{letter-image|Proto-semiticA-01.svg}}
** Proto-Canaanite: {{letter-image|Protoalef.svg}}
*** {{desctree|phn|𐤀|sclb=1|tr=-}}
** {{desctree|sem-srb|𐩱|sclb=1|tr=-}}
** {{desc|xna|𐪑|sclb=1|tr=-}}
{{bottom}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|461}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the head and neck of an animal.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>F10</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ꜥm||to swallow}}.}} {{defdate|Late Period}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḫḫ||throat}} and words involving activities of the throat, e.g. {{m|egy|ꜥm||to swallow}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|462}}
* {{R:egy:Wb|1|183}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>A ligature of <hiero>rnp</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓆳}}) and <hiero>wp</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓄋}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>F14</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|wp-rnpt||New Year’s Day}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|462}}
* {{R:egy:Wb}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>A ligature of <hiero>rnp</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓆳}}), <hiero>ra</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓇳}}), and <hiero>wp</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓄋}}), or equivalently of <hiero>F14</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓄍}}) and <hiero>ra</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓇳}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>F15</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|wp-rnpt||New Year’s Day}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|462}}
* {{R:egy:Wb}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a mammal’s belly (with teats) and tail, viewed from the underside. The phonetic value of {{m|egy||ẖ}} is derived by the rebus principle from its use as the logogram for {{m|egy|ẖt||belly}}. The color of this glyph when painted greatly varied.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>X</hiero>|tr=ẖ}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ẖ}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ẖt||belly, body}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|465}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a sheep’s heart in cross-section; the neck and protrusions represent the junctures of the arteries and veins. The glyph is typically painted red, sometimes with a lighter upper part. A variant form without any interior markings or exterior protrusions (essentially a simple oval) was often used as a determinative of {{m|egy|ḥꜣtj}} in the Old Kingdom. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|心|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>ib</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jb||heart}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḥꜣtj||heart}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|465}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|25}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a sheep's heart and windpipe or esophagus in cross-section. The heart section shows the same color variations as {{m|egy|𓄣}} (Gardiner F34), whilst the windpipe/esophagus section may be white with red outline, or alternatively green, possibly a symbol of life-giving breath.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>nfr</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[phonogram|Phonogram]] for {{m|egy|nfr}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|nfr||perfect, complete, good, beautiful, {{w|nefer}}}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|465}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Hieroglyphs from the tomb of Seti I.jpg|right|thumb|Colorful hieroglyphs from the tomb of Seti I, showing 𓄿.]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing the [[Egyptian vulture]] ({{taxfmt|Neophron percnopterus|species}}). The sharp “corner” at the back of the head represents a tuft of feathers sticking out; other details sometimes shown include a featherless area at the base of the beak and a lean, thinly feathered neck. {{egy-hiero-color|polychrome|2=y}} The oldest depictions color the wings blue or grey, but later they become green; the glyph as a whole is polychrome, with a largely yellow head, and yellow or red feet. (Less careful representations are sometimes all yellow, all blue, or nearly all white.) The phonetic value of {{m|egy||ꜣ}} is derived by the rebus principle from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ꜣ||Egyptian vulture, bird}}. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|鳥|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>A</hiero>|tr=ꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ꜣ||Egyptian vulture, bird}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph should not be confused with the extremely similar <hiero>tyw</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅂}}, a buzzard), which serves as a phonogram for {{m|egy||tjw}} and has a more rounded back of the head. In some inscriptions the two could also be distinguished by their colors, the buzzard being largely yellow with red details.</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|467}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* Nina Davies, ''Picture Writing in Ancient Egypt'' (1958): "Pl. IV, 1. Egyptian Vulture. Phonetic ꜣ 'vulture' (G1). From the tomb of Rekhmire (no. 100). White ground. Although the bird in outline differs little from the long-legged buzzard (G4, our Pl. IV, 3) with which the Egyptians sometimes confused it, there is no resemblance in the painted examples and these do not vary." [Plate depicts the vulture wih blue-green wings and mostly yellow head and feet, the buzzard entirely yellow with red feather-details on the wings and tail.]
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)

𓄿.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|467}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* Nina Davies, ''Picture Writing in Ancient Egypt'' (1958): "Pl. IV, 1. Egyptian Vulture. Phonetic ꜣ 'vulture' (G1). From the tomb of Rekhmire (no. 100). White ground. Although the bird in outline differs little from the long-legged buzzard (G4, our Pl. IV, 3) with which the Egyptians sometimes confused it, there is no resemblance in the painted examples and these do not vary." [Plate depicts the vulture wih blue-green wings and mostly yellow head and feet, the buzzard entirely yellow with red feather-details on the wings and tail.]
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[falcon]], variously identified as a [[lanner falcon]] or [[peregrine falcon]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>G5</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ḥr||Horus}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|occasionally}} {{alt form|egy|𓅆}} (<hiero>G7</hiero>). {{defdate|chiefly Old Kingdom and Late Period}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|467}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|26}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a falcon with a [[flagellum]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>G6</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|bjk||falcon}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|468}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an owl. This is the only bird hieroglyph that is depicted face-on, perhaps because of its distinctive gaze. The reversible hind toe is not drawn. The precise species of owl varied by time period; in the Protodynastic Period it was the pharaoh [[eagle owl]] ({{taxlink|Bubo ascalaphus|species}}), and this was also used sporadically throughout dynastic times. Since the 3rd Dynasty the owl was more commonly a [[barn owl]] ({{taxlink|Tyto alba alba|subspecies}}). In hieratic the owl is long-eared (‘horned’) since the earliest times. Conventionally, it is colored yellow.

The phonetic value of {{m|egy||m}} is presumably derived from some word (probably for an owl), but this word may have been lost in archaic times; numerous hypotheses have been put forward about what it might have been:

* A Proto-Egyptian {{m|egy|*mwlḏ||owl}}, cf. {{noncog|egx-dem|ꜣmwlḏ}}, {{m|egx-dem|ꜥmwlḏ||eagle owl}}
* An {{noncog|egy-old|*mz||owl}}, cf. {{noncog|ha|mujiya|múúǯììyáá|owl}}, {{noncog|gwn|múǯìya||owl}}
* An {{noncog|egy-old|*jmw||owl|lit=the mourner}}, cf. {{noncog|egy|jm||lament, mourn}}, {{noncog|egy-lat|jm||name of a bird}}
* An {{noncog|egy-old|*mꜣ||owl|lit=the seeing one}}, cf. {{noncog|egy|mꜣꜣ||to see}}
* Related to Semitic {{m|sem-pro|*būm-||owl}}

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>m</hiero>|tr=m}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||m}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:EDE|volume=3|head=m}}
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|469}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing two owls, the hieroglyph <hiero>m</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅓}}, phonogram for {{m|egy||m}}) reduplicated.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>mm</hiero>|tr=mm}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||mm}}, as in the preposition {{m|egy|mm||among}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|jm||therein}}.}} {{defdate|18th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|469}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Originally representing the [[saddle-billed stork]] ({{taxfmt|Ephippiorhynchus senegalensis|species}}). The stork is depicted with decreasing accuracy over time; the earliest instances come from knife and mace handles from the Late [[Predynastic Period]] and are very accurate. Starting from the 3rd Dynasty the bill of the hieroglyphic stork began to shorten. In later phases of the [[Old Kingdom]], as the bird died out and disappeared from Egypt, the glyph became increasingly schematized, with a shorter neck and bill, a goose-like posture, and incorrect placement of the [[wattle]] on the neck or chest rather than at the base of the bill. This form then continued on mostly unchanged for the rest of Egyptian history.

The phonetic value of {{m|egy||bꜣ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as a logogram for {{m|egy|bꜣ||[[ba]]}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>G29</hiero>|tr=bꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||bꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|bꜣ||[[ba]]}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This is both the earliest and most widespread glyph to serve as a logogram for {{m|egy|bꜣ}}, among <hiero>E10</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓃝}}), <hiero>G53</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅽}}), and possibly <hiero>F9</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓄇}}) with the same meaning.</div>

===References===
* Janák, Jíří (2014) ''Saddle-Billed Stork (Ba-Bird)'' in the ''UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology''.
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|470}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

𓅡.references:
* Janák, Jíří (2014) ''Saddle-Billed Stork (Ba-Bird)'' in the ''UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology''.
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|470}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a waterfowl of uncertain species, likely the same as that referred to by the word {{m|egy|ꜣpd}} in its stricter sense, or perhaps simply an abstraction intended to represent a prototypical waterfowl (and therefore bird). Gardiner considered it the [[white-fronted goose]] ({{taxfmt|Anser albifrons|species}}), but only one existing example of this hieroglyph shows details that certainly establish it to be of this species, so this may be attributable to an idiosyncrasy of a particular scribe. Other authors instead argue that the glyph is a duck on the basis of Faulkner’s arguments regarding the original meaning of {{m|egy|ꜣpd}}.

The phonetic value of {{m|egy||gb}} is derived by the rebus principle from its use as the determinative for a kind of goose, {{m|egy|gbb}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>G38</hiero>|tr=gb}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||gb}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ꜣpd||bird}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for kinds of waterfowl, as in {{m|egy|ꜣpd}}, {{m|egy|rꜣ}}, {{m|egy|ṯrp}}, {{m|egy|gbb}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|in hieratic texts}} {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for birds and insects in general.}}
# {{ng|[[phonetic determinative|Phonetic determinative]] in various verbs: {{m|egy|wfꜣ||to discuss}}, {{m|egy|wzf||to be(come) idle}}, {{m|egy|wdfj||to be(come) late}}, {{m|egy|ḥtm||to perish}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph should not be confused with the extremely similar <hiero>G39</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅭}}), which serves as a phonogram for {{m|egy||zꜣ}} and depicts a pintail duck. However, in practice, it is sometimes impossible to tell which bird is represented; in such cases, either glyph can be chosen to transcribe the original.</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|471}}
* Yongsheng, Chen (2016) “The Prototypical Determinatives in Egyptian and Chinese Writing” in ''Scripta: International Journal of Writing Systems'', volume 8, page 109
* Faulkner, Raymond (1952) “''ꜣpd'' = ‘duck’” in ''The Journal of Egyptian Archaeology'', Vol. 38, p. 128

𓅬.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|471}}
* Yongsheng, Chen (2016) “The Prototypical Determinatives in Egyptian and Chinese Writing” in ''Scripta: International Journal of Writing Systems'', volume 8, page 109
* Faulkner, Raymond (1952) “''ꜣpd'' = ‘duck’” in ''The Journal of Egyptian Archaeology'', Vol. 38, p. 128

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a quail chick. In semi-cursive hieroglyphic writing the head tends to be summarized as a hook. {{egy-hiero-color|yellow}} The source of its phonetic value is unknown.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>w</hiero>|tr=w}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||w}}, as in the plural ending {{m|egy|-w}}.}}

====See also====
* {{l|egy|-w}}
* {{l|egy|𓏲}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|472}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing the head of a pintail duck <hiero>zA</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅭}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>H1</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ꜣpd|ꜣpdw|fowl}} in the [[offering formula]].}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|wšn||wring the neck of (a bird), offer}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|in hieratic texts}} {{ng|[[phonetic determinative|Phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||mꜣꜥ}}, by conflation with <hiero>H2</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓆀}}) when it lacks a crest.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|473}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the head of a crested [[heron]], although possibly originally of a duck, given that there is a word for duck {{m|egy|mꜣꜥ}}, in which case the phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||mꜣꜥ}} could derive from that word.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>wSm</hiero>|tr=wšm, pꜣq, mꜣꜥ}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] or [[phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||wšm}}, as in {{m|egy|wšm||ear of corn}}.}}
# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]], replacing <hiero>pAq</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓆁}}), for {{m|egy||pꜣq}} in {{m|egy|pꜣqt||fine linen}}.}} {{defdate|since the 12th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|[[phonetic determinative|Phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||mꜣꜥ}}, as in {{m|egy|mꜣꜥ||temple}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the head of a [[spoonbill]] ({{taxfmt|Platalea leucorodia|species}}).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>pAq</hiero>|tr=pꜣq}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||pꜣq}}, as in {{m|egy|pꜣq||a type of cake}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing the head of the vulture <hiero>G14</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅐}}, {{taxfmt|Gyps fulvus|species}}). The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||nr}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the word for vulture, {{m|egy|nrt}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>nr</hiero>|tr=nr}}

# {{ng|[[phonetic determinative|Phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||nr}}.}}
# {{ng|Used as a [[cryptograph]] for {{m|egy|rmṯ||people}}.}} {{defdate|since the 18th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a bird’s wing. Compare {{noncog|zh|羽}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>H5</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for wings and flight.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a white ostrich feather. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||šw}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as the logogram for {{m|egy|šwt||feather}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>Sw</hiero>|tr=šw}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||šw}}, as in {{m|egy|šw||Shu}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|šwt||feather}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|mꜣꜥt||[[Maat]]}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|mꜣꜥt||[[Maat]]}} as a substitute for <hiero>C10</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓁦}}).}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>This sign does not actually exist in hieroglyphic text; it is an artificial glyph used for transcribing hieratic text. It represents the hieratic version of a white ostrich feather <hiero>H6</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓆄}}) with two strokes at the side.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>H6A</hiero>|tr=šw}}

# {{lb|egy|Middle Egyptian}} {{form of|egy|Hieratic form|𓆄}} (<hiero>H6</hiero>) {{ng|as a [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||šw}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Abydos Tempel Ramses II. 37 b.jpg|right|thumb]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a bird’s clawed foot.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>H7</hiero>|tr=šꜣt}}

# {{rfv-sense|egy}} {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||šꜣt}}, as in {{m|egy|šꜣt||talon; land of Shat}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|pḏ||span}} in the units of measurement {{m|egy|pḏ ꜥꜣ}} and {{m|egy|pḏ nḏs}}.}} {{defdate|New Kingdom}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|jꜣft||talon}}.}} {{defdate|Pyramid Texts}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an egg. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|卵|sc=Hani}}. As a determinative in {{m|egy|pꜥt||patricians}}, it may have originally been a different glyph, a clod of earth. {{egy-hiero-color|white|nodot=1}} or yellow,[[Category:Yellow Egyptian hieroglyphs]] reflecting the creamy color of ostrich or goose eggs.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>H8</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{lb|egy|in hieratic texts}} {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|zꜣ||son}} by conflation with <hiero>zA</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓅭}})}} {{defdate|19th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|swḥt||egg}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in names of goddesses.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|pꜥt||patricians}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|474}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an abstracted, universalized lizard; from the New Kingdom on a gecko is depicted instead. The phonogrammatic value derives by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ꜥšꜣ||lizard}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>aSA</hiero>|tr=ꜥšꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||ꜥšꜣ}}, as in {{m|egy|ꜥšꜣ||to be numerous}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ꜥšꜣ||lizard}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words for lizards, as in {{m|egy|ḥntꜣsw||lizard}}, {{m|egy|ꜥšꜣ||lizard}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a freshwater turtle.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I2</hiero>|tr=štw}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|štw||freshwater turtle}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|štw||freshwater turtle}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the [[Nile crocodile]] ({{taxlink|Crocodylus niloticus|species}}). The reason for the phonetic value {{m|egy||jt}} is obscure, but Gardiner suggests it could come from the word’s use as a determinative in {{m|egy|ꜣd||to be angry or aggressive}} as sound changes brought this word closer in pronunciation to {{m|egy||jt}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I3</hiero>|tr=jt}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||jt}} in {{m|egy|jty||sovereign}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|mzḥ||crocodile}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḫntj|pos=an epithet of the god Set as a crocodile}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words relating to [[crocodile]]s and their perceived qualities: {{m|egy|ḥntj||to be greedy}}, {{m|egy|skn||greedy}}, {{m|egy|ꜥẖm|pos=name of a demon|lit=Horrifier}}, {{m|egy|ꜣd||to be angry or aggressive}}, etc.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a [[crocodile]] on a [[shrine]]. Older variants lack the shrine and instead depict the crocodile as a cult image (fetish) or a mummified crocodile, with only its head emerging from a wrapping of bandages or a [[sudarium]]: {{m|egy|𓆍}} (<hiero>I5A</hiero>).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I4</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|sbk||[[Sobek]]}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|sbk||[[Sobek]]}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[crocodile]] with an inward curved [[tail]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>sAq</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|sꜣq||to gather or contract together}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|sꜣq||to gather or contract together}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a cult image (fetish) or a mummified crocodile, with only its head emerging from a wrapping of bandages or a [[sudary]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I5A</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{alt form|egy|𓆋}} (<hiero>I4</hiero>)

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing [[crocodile]] hide with [[spines]]. The Old Kingdom form is rounded, with the spikes rising from the curving top, {{egy-h|I6A}}, while the later form that became usual in the Middle Kingdom shows a more squarish piece of hide with spikes at its left side. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||km}} possibly derives from the glyph’s use in {{m|egy|jkm||shield}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|km}}|tr=km}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||km}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[frog#Animal|frog]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I7</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|wḥm-ꜥnḫ||(cyclically) repeating life}}.}} {{defdate|since the 18th or 19th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḥqt||Heqat}}, and in words for frog: {{m|egy|qrr||frog}}, {{m|egy|ꜥbḫn||frog}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[tadpole]]. Because of phonetic similarity, and because Egyptian wetlands were overrun with them, the tadpole ({{m|egy|ḥfnr}}) became a logogram for {{m|egy|ḥfn||hundred thousand}}, representing underlying {{m|egy||ḥfl}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>Hfn</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḥfn||hundred thousand}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḥfnr||tadpole}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|475}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[horned]] [[viper]]. The hieratic ''Treatise on Ophiology'' provides a description: ‘Its color is similar to that of the quail; it has two horns on its forehead; the head is broad, the neck narrow, and the tail thick.’ {{egy-hiero-color|yellow}} The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||f}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the word for a viper, {{m|egy|ft}} (Demotic {{m|egx-dem|fy}}).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>f</hiero>|tr=f}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||f}}, as in {{m|egy|.f}} and {{m|egy|f}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ft||horned viper}}.}}
# {{ng|Used with unclear signification in the word {{m|egy|jt||father}}.}}

