===Glyph origin===
Representing an [[erect]] [[cobra]] ([[uraeus]]).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I12</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|jꜥrt||uraeus}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in the names of goddesses and in the plural of {{m|egy|nṯrt||goddess}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|476}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Ligature of {{m|egy|𓆗}} and {{m|egy|𓎠}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I13</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for goddesses.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|476}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[snake]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>I14</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for snakes and worms, as in {{m|egy|ḥfꜣw||snake}} or {{m|egy|ḏdft||snake, worm}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|476}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Vessel in the Form of a Fish LACMA AC1999.57.1.jpg|right|thumb|A vessel in the shape of the fish hieroglyph.]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a fish with prominent fins, probably a [[Nile tilapia]] ({{m|egy|jnt}}). Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|魚|sc=Hani}} ("fish") and {{noncog|emy|-}} [[File:Maya Syllabary Ka 3.jpg|30px]] (''ka'', "fish"<!--; whence e.g. {{cog|yua|kay}}-->).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>K1</hiero>|tr=jn}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] for {{m|egy||jn}}.}}
# {{ng|Determinative used in the words for various fish.}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an [[oxyrhynchus]] fish ({{taxlink|Mormyrus kannume|species}}). The distinctive long snout separates it from other fish hieroglyphs. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ẖꜣ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ẖꜣt||oxyrhynchus}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>XA</hiero>|tr=ẖꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ẖꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ẖꜣt||oxyrhynchus}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|477}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[scarab]] beetle ({{taxfmt|Scarabaeus sacer|species}}). The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ḫpr}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ḫprr||scarab beetle}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>xpr</hiero>|tr=ḫpr}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||ḫpr}}, as in {{m|egy|ḫpr||to come into being}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫprr||scarab beetle}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḫprr||scarab beetle}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|477}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a bee. The hind leg (drawn raised under the abdomen) was often omitted. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||bjt}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|bjt||bee; honey}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>bit</hiero>|tr=bjt}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||bjt}}, as in {{m|egy|bjtj||King of Lower Egypt}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|bjt||bee; honey}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|477}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Ligature of <hiero>sw</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓇓}}), <hiero>t</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓏏}}), <hiero>bit</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓆤}}), and <hiero>t</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓏏}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>sw:t-bit:t</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|nswt-bjtj||King of Upper and Lower Egypt}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner}}
* {{R:egy:Wb}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the desert locust ({{taxlink|Schistocerca gregaria|species}}).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>L4</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|znḥm||locust}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|477}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the red-headed centipede ({{taxlink|Scolopendra morsitans|species}}).

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>L5</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|zpꜣ||centipede}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|478}}
* Houlihan, Patrick F. (2002) “Animals in Egyptian art and hieroglyphics” in ''A history of the animal world in the ancient Near East'' (ed. Collins, Billie Jean), page 142

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a bivalve shell. The source of its phonetic value is unknown.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>L6</hiero>|tr=ḫꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||ḫꜣ}}, restricted in Middle Egyptian to use in {{m|egy|ḫꜣwt||altar}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|478}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
[[File:Kingscorpion.jpg|300px|thumb|At right, the earlier form of the scorpion glyph.]]
<div>Representing a scorpion. The glyph as usually depicted lacks its tail and venomous stinger for superstitious reasons; this usage began in funerary texts in burial chambers but in time became standard. However, above-ground Old Kingdom examples typically show the scorpion intact, {{egy-h|L19}}. Some of the earliest (intact) depictions are shown in profile, with all the legs on the underside; later the legs were placed on both sides, representing a more top-down view.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|L7}}|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|srqt||the goddess [[Serqet]]}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for the name of {{w|King Scorpion}} (reading uncertain).}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|478}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[trunk]] or [[branch]]. Old Kingdom examples show a tree trunk with all of its knots and branches lopped off but the one at the very end. Later New Kingdom examples instead depict a branch with twigs. The phonogrammatic value derives by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ḫt||wood}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>xt</hiero>|tr=ḫt}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḫt}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫt||wood}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words for types of wood, things made of wood, or the woody parts of plants.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|479}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Darius II cartouche at Hibis d1.jpg|thumb|upright|[[cartouche]] of {{w|Darius II}}, with the final -š of the name visible]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[pool]] with [[lotus]] flowers. The phonogrammatic value derives by the [[rebus principle]] from the glyph’s use as a logogram for {{m|egy|šꜣ||field, countryside, swamp}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>M8</hiero>|tr=šꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||šꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|Group-writing [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||š}}; compare [[uniliteral]] {{m|egy|𓈙|}} (Gardiner number: N37).}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|šꜣ||field, countryside, swamp}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ꜣḫt||[[Akhet]], [[inundation]] season}}.}}

