|}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|480}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a highly stylized [[papyrus]] [[stem]] with [[umbel]]. Archaic forms of the glyph show a sheath of tall projecting leaves at the base of the stem as well as narrow outer leaves at the base of the umbel; occasionally this persisted even into the Old Kingdom and later. {{egy-hiero-color|green}} The phonogrammatic value derives by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|wꜣḏ||papyrus stem}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>wAD</hiero>|tr=wꜣḏ, wḏ}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||wꜣḏ}}, as in {{m|egy|wꜣḏ||to be(come) green}}.}}
# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||wḏ}} by conflation with <hiero>wD</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓎗}}), as in {{m|egy|swḏ||to bequeath}}.}} {{defdate|Since the Middle Kingdom}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|wꜣḏ||papyrus stem}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|480}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a flowering reed, specifically the [[panicle]] of {{taxfmt|Phragmites australis|species}}.<ref>Täckholm, Vivi; Täckholm, Gunnar; Drar, Mohammed (1941) ''Flora of Egypt''</ref> From the Old Kingdom on the flowering portion was often represented as striated. Joints in the stem were rarely depicted in the Old Kingdom but later became more frequent. The main (flowering or frond) portion was conventionally colored green, and the stem blue, so consistently that this glyph can be used to tell blue from green on polychrome inscriptions where the paint is discolored or faded.<ref>David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (Université Libre de Bruxelles - Faculté de Philosophie et Sciences sociales, 2020)</ref> The phonetic value of {{m|egy||j}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|j||reed}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>i</hiero>|tr=j}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||j}}, including as the suffix pronoun {{m|egy|.j}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|j||reed}}.}}
# {{ng|Used as part of <hiero>i-i</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓇌}}).}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|481}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
<references/>

[[Category:Green Egyptian hieroglyphs]]
[[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]]

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the [[sky]] with an architectural rather than mythological image, as a gigantic ceiling resting on four supports. The phonogrammatic value is derived from the glyph’s use in {{m|egy|ḥrt||sky}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>pt</hiero>|tr=ḥrj}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] or [[biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḥrj}} or {{m|egy||ḥr}}, as in {{m|egy|ḥrj||being above}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|pt||sky}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḥrt||sky}} in {{m|egy|jn-ḥrt}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words for the sky, as in {{m|egy|nwt}}, {{m|egy|ḥrt}}, {{m|egy|pt}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words for gates, by confusion with other glyphs, as in {{m|egy|rwtj||double gate}}, {{m|egy|hꜣyt||portal}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|485}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing the [[sky]] with an uncertain object suspended from it, originally {{m|egy|𓇱}} (<hiero>N3</hiero>), with the object possibly a broken [[oar]] or a drill <hiero>U25</hiero>, <hiero>U24</hiero> with the sky as drill-weight. In later versions from the 18th Dynasty the object looks similar to the lower half of a broken scepter <hiero>wAs</hiero>.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N2</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|grḥ||night}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to darkness, obscurity, and night, as in {{m|egy|grḥ}}, {{m|egy|wḫ||night}}, {{m|egy|kkw||darkness}}, {{m|egy|jḫḫw||dusk}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|485}}
* {{R:egy:Wb|5|183}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing the [[sky]] with an uncertain object suspended from it, possibly a broken [[oar]] or a drill <hiero>U25</hiero>, <hiero>U24</hiero> with the sky as drill-weight. A later variant of this glyph that replaced it is <hiero>N2</hiero>.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N3</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{alt form|egy|𓇰}} (<hiero>N2</hiero>). {{defdate|Old Kingdom}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|485}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the sky with water descending from it. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|雨|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>idt</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jꜣdt||dew}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for precipitation, dew, and fog.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|jꜣdw||pestilence}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|485}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
A pictogram [[File:Red Circle(small).svg|16px]] of the sun. The circle or dot in the center usually does not appear in the full hieroglyphic form, which is a filled, usually convex circle colored red, often with a white rim. Compare the Chinese character [[File:日-bronze.svg|16px|link=日]] and the modern astronomical solar symbol [[☉]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>ra</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|rꜥ||sun, day}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|hrw||day}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|sw||day of the month}} in dates.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to the sun, its actions, and related phenomena.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to time.}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>ra:N23</hiero>, itself a [[determinative]] for time.}} {{defdate|11th|12th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|485}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

