etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the sun with a [[uraeus]]. From the 18th Dynasty.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N6</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|rꜥ||sun}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|rꜥ||sun}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|486}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing the crescent moon. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|月|sc=Hani}}. Variants appear vertically, facing left {{egy-h|N11y}} or right {{egy-h|N11z}}; another variant is {{egy-h|N12}} ({{m|egy|𓇺}}). The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ꜥḥ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|jꜥḥ||moon}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|iaH}}|tr=ꜥḥ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] or [[phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||ꜥḥ}} in {{m|egy|wꜥḥ||carob beans}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jꜥḥ||moon}}.}}
# {{ng|Alone or in combination as {{egy-h|iaH:sbA}}, [[logogram]] for {{m|egy|ꜣbd||month}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|šzp||palm (measure)}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for the moon, as in {{m|egy|jꜥḥ||moon}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|šzp||palm (measure)}}.}}
# {{ng|Alternative form of {{egy-h|spr}} ({{m|egy|𓄭}}) in some inscriptions.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|486}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>A combination of {{m|egy|𓇼}} (<hiero>sbA</hiero>) with the right half of {{m|egy|𓇹}} (<hiero>iaH</hiero>), indicating half a month.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N13</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|smdt||half-month festival}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|486}}
* {{R:egy:Wb|4|147}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[star]] in the form of a five-pointed [[starfish]]. Older depictions are thicker and have rounded ends, and they sometimes show the center as two concentric circles and give the arms crossbars. In color the glyph reflects the colors of the stars and so can be yellow, gold, red, and, less commonly, blue. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||sbꜣ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|sbꜣ||star}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>sbA</hiero>|tr=sbꜣ, dwꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||sbꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]]<!--radicogram--> for {{m|egy||dwꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|sbꜣ||star}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|wnwt||hour}}.}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>iaH:sbA</hiero>, a [[logogram]] for {{m|egy|ꜣbd||month}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for stars and constellations, as in {{m|egy|sbꜣ||star}}, {{m|egy|msḫtjw||the Big Dipper, the Plough}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|wnwt||hour}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|487}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a star {{m|egy|𓇼}} (<hiero>dwA</hiero>) in a circle, symbolically representing the world of the dead, which was in the oldest times associated with the places where the sun and stars reappeared after having been invisible.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>dwAt</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|dwꜣt||the Afterworld}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|dwꜣt||the Afterworld}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|487}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>This was originally three different glyphs: one representing a sandy tract of land (an island in the Nile or a sandy hill in the flood plain, {{egy-h|N18}}), one representing a bolt of rolled-up linen ({{egy-h|S208}}, variant {{egy-h|S209}}), and one representing a roll of bread ({{egy-h|X4I}}, variant {{egy-h|X4}} ({{m|egy|𓏒}})). The land glyph is generally colored reddish yellow and sometimes speckled to indicate sand.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N18</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{lb|egy|group-writing}} {{ng|The group {{egy-h|N18:N21*Z1}} constitutes a [[uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||j}}, as in {{m|egy|jrt||Yareth}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jw||island}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for deserts and foreign countries, as in {{m|egy|ꜣḫt||place of sunrise/sunset}}, {{m|egy|ṯḥnw||Libya}}, {{m|egy|stt||Asia}}.}}
# {{alt form|egy|𓋰}} ({{egy-h|S208}})
# {{alt form|egy|𓏒}} ({{egy-h|X4}})

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|487}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a tongue of land. This glyph developed from the earlier <hiero>N22</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈆}}). The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||wḏb}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use in {{m|egy|wḏb||sandbank}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>wDb</hiero>|tr=wḏb}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||wḏb}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|wḏb||sandbank, shore}} and {{m|egy|ḥꜣb-sd||''sd'' festival}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|488}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a tongue of land or perhaps a standard cultivated parcel (surveyed parcels for farming were usually shaped like elongated trapezoids, with the long sides parallel to the Nile). Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|田|sc=Hani}}. This glyph originally developed as a variant of the earlier <hiero>N22</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈆}}). In the 8th Dynasty <hiero>N36</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈘}}) came to be used instead as the determinative for cultivated land, and in the 11th Dynasty this developed into <hiero>N23</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈇}}), but in the 18th Dynasty <hiero>N21</hiero> became more widely used and took their place as the ordinary determinative for this purpose.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N21</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jdb||riverbank}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for land, particularly cultivated land near water, as in {{m|egy|jdb||riverbank}}, {{m|egy|tꜣ||land, ground}}, {{m|egy|dmj||town, harbor}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|488}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|35}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a sandy tongue of land. The sand is sometimes represented as speckles across the glyph. This was the Old Kingdom precursor to both <hiero>N20</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈄}}) and <hiero>N21</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈅}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N22</hiero>|tr=wḏb}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||wḏb}}.}} {{defdate|Old Kingdom}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|wḏb||sandbank, shore}}.}} {{defdate|Old Kingdom}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for land, as in {{m|egy|ꜥḥt||field}}.}} {{defdate|Old Kingdom}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|488}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing an irrigation canal. In hieroglyphic text, this glyph developed from a variant of <hiero>N36</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈘}}) in the 11th Dynasty, which in turn had partly supplanted <hiero>N22</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈆}}), its variants <hiero>N21</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈅}}) and <hiero>N20</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈄}}), and <hiero>Aa12</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓐚}}) in the 8th Dynasty. However, in hieratic, a glyph much like this one was already in use since the Old Kingdom. Ultimately, the glyph <hiero>N23</hiero> remained in use through the 18th Dynasty, when it was largely again supplanted by <hiero>N21</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈅}}) as a determinative for land.</div>

<div>As part of a determinative for time, this glyph instead developed from <hiero>Z4B</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓐅}}), which became conflated with the irrigation-canal glyph.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N23</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for irrigated land, as in {{m|egy|tꜣ||land}}.}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>ra:N23</hiero>, a [[determinative]] for time, as in {{m|egy|tr||season}}, {{m|egy|rk||time}}.}} {{defdate|11th|12th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>mw-N36:N23</hiero>, a composite [[determinative]] for bodies of water.}} {{defdate|since the 18th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|488, 490}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|35}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing hilly areas beyond the Nile River Valley. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|山|sc=Hani}}. The hieroglyph was generally colored yellow, reddish, or pink with dark spots to represent desert and rock, and sometimes its base was colored with a black or green line to represent cultivated land. Archaic forms (and occasionally Old Kingdom and later forms) show the outer sides sloping instead of vertical.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>xAst</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫꜣst||foreign land, desert}}.}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>xAst:R12</hiero>, a [[logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḥꜣ#Etymology 3|ḥꜣ|the god Ha}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for wild or desert places.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for names of foreign countries.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|488}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|36}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing two hills framing a [[wadi]]. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|丘|sc=Hani}}. The hieroglyph was generally colored yellow or pink with dark spots to represent desert and rock, and sometimes its base was colored black or green to represent cultivated land. Archaic forms (and occasionally Old Kingdom and later forms) show the outer sides sloping instead of vertical. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ḏw}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ḏw||mountain}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>Dw</hiero>|tr=ḏw}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḏw}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḏw||mountain}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|mntj||the two mountain ranges}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for names of foreign countries, by confusion with <hiero>xAst</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈉}}).}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|36}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the sun rising or setting between two mountains. This sign first appeared during the 5th Dynasty. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|旦|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>Axt</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ꜣḫt||place of sunrise or sunset}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing (probably) the sun rising behind a hill. The inner curve represents the hill, while the outer curve represents the rays of the sun. Old Kingdom examples show a semicircular form with four colorful bands of blues, reds, and greens surmounted by a halo of rays, {{egy-h|N28A}}; Betrò suggests it may thus represent a rainbow rather than a hill. Allen suggests that it represents the sun rising above the primeval mound, the first earth to arise from the primordial waters at the time of creation. The semi-cursive style of writing this hieroglyph, which showed the sun’s rays as a series of projections instead of an unbroken arc, {{egy-h|N28D}}, was sometimes carried over into ordinary hieroglyphic writing. Another form shows the arc unbroken but rays still visible, {{egy-h|N28E}}. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ḫꜥ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ḫꜥ}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|xa}}|tr=ḫꜥ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḫꜥ}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫꜥ||hill where the sun rises{{sup|?}}, rainbow{{sup|?}}}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫꜥj||to appear, to rise}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|36}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Allen|p=131}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a hill. Old Kingdom examples are fairly triangular. The glyph is usually colored (dark) blue, although white, yellow, and red are also found<!-- (some inscriptions even color different instances differently)-->. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||q}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from the word for a hill, {{m|egy|qꜣꜣ}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>q</hiero>|tr=q}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||q}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|qꜣꜣ||hill}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|13}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)<!-- "This sign is highly variable, showing mostly blue (as here) or white. This therefore cannot be a sandy hill slope (although Gardiner cites one reference - EG 489). Blue or black would normally indicate alluvial land, not a hill. So this sign remains enigmatic (see the commentary on N29)." [...] "The hill slope N29 is described by Gardiner as “sandy” (EG 489). However, sand would be represented by pink, red or yellow with dark spots, as for instance in N27. The occurrences found here are mostly blue, with white, yellow and red as alternatives. In one inscription (Mon.31), two colours, blue and red, have been used in the same context. Blue often represents the alluvium left by the receding Nile flood. Yellow and red are desert colours. White can represent limestone. It would appear then, that we have a prototypical hillside. This is unusual in the canon, where hieroglyphs are normally categorised by the use of the same or similar colours. The different shades used here remain, at this time, somewhat obscure." -->

[[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]]

𓈎.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|13}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)<!-- "This sign is highly variable, showing mostly blue (as here) or white. This therefore cannot be a sandy hill slope (although Gardiner cites one reference - EG 489). Blue or black would normally indicate alluvial land, not a hill. So this sign remains enigmatic (see the commentary on N29)." [...] "The hill slope N29 is described by Gardiner as “sandy” (EG 489). However, sand would be represented by pink, red or yellow with dark spots, as for instance in N27. The occurrences found here are mostly blue, with white, yellow and red as alternatives. In one inscription (Mon.31), two colours, blue and red, have been used in the same context. Blue often represents the alluvium left by the receding Nile flood. Yellow and red are desert colours. White can represent limestone. It would appear then, that we have a prototypical hillside. This is unusual in the canon, where hieroglyphs are normally categorised by the use of the same or similar colours. The different shades used here remain, at this time, somewhat obscure." -->

[[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]]

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a mound of earth lined with shrubs.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>iAt</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jꜣt||mound}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|jꜣt||mound}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a road lined with shrubs. The depicted road tended to be curved in the early phases of the Old Kingdom, and such curved variants occasionally appeared even much later, when the most common form had become straight. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ḥr}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use in {{m|egy|ḥrt||road}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>N31</hiero>|tr=ḥr}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḥr}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|wꜣt||road}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|wꜣj||to fall into (a condition)}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to roads, travel, position, and distance.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|489}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|36}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==
{{wikipedia}}

===Glyph origin===
A depiction of waves. The number of ripples varies, but the ends always slant down and can sometimes be longer than the other strokes. In less careful writing, the hieroglyph can be found simplified to a single horizontal line, sometimes rising at the end. Rarely, it can be turned on its side to stand vertically. {{egy-hiero-color|black|nodot=y}}<!-- or grey? [source?]-->, or dark blue,[[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]] suggestive of silt-laden Nile flood water which watered the "black land" ({{m|egy|kmt}}). (Compare the canal glyph {{m|egy|𓈘}}, where the water is most often green, and the pool glyph {{m|egy|𓈙}}, where the water was lighter blue or green.) Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|巛|sc=Hani}}. 

The phonogrammatic value is possibly derived by the [[rebus principle]] from {{m|egy|nt||water}}, but this word only appears in the Middle Kingdom. An alternative source could be the older {{m|egy|nwyt||swell of water}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>n</hiero>|tr=n}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||n}}, as for example in the preposition {{m|egy|n||to, for}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḏt||serf}}, by confusion with <hiero>N17</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓇿}}).}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>mw</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈗}}).}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|490}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|11}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

𓈖.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|490}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|11}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a canal bordered by banks and dikes. In detailed Old Kingdom depictions the water area is covered with zigzagging ripples, {{egy-h|N36A}}. Sometimes the canal is shown curved rather than straight, {{egy-h|N100}}, especially in the Old Kingdom. This glyph partly supplanted {{egy-h|N22}} ({{m|egy|𓈆}}), its variants {{egy-h|N21}} ({{m|egy|𓈅}}) and {{egy-h|N20}} ({{m|egy|𓈄}}), and {{egy-h|Aa12}} ({{m|egy|𓐚}}) as a determinative for cultivated land in the 8th Dynasty, but in this function eventually changed into the form {{egy-h|N23}} ({{m|egy|𓈇}}) in the 11th Dynasty. The water in the canal is conventionally green, or sometimes (lighter) blue; the outline is (darker) blue, perhaps representing mud brick.[[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]][[Category:Green Egyptian hieroglyphs]] The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||mr}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|mr||canal}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|N36}}|tr=mr, mj}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||mr}}.}}
# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||mj}}, as in {{m|egy|mjzt||liver}}, {{m|egy|mꜥḥꜥt|mjꜥḥꜥt|tomb}}.}} {{defdate|since the 18th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|mr||canal}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for bodies of water, interchanging with {{m|egy|𓈙}} ({{egy-h|S}}) in early times.}}
# {{ng|Used in {{egy-h|mw-N36:N23}} or {{egy-h|mw-N36}}, a composite [[determinative]] for bodies of water.}} {{defdate|since the 18th Dynasty}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for irrigated land.}} {{defdate|8th–11th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|491}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|35–36}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

𓈘.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|491}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|35–36}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a lake or pool. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|𣶒|sc=Hani}}. Detailed Old Kingdom forms cover the interior with vertical zigzags of green or blue rippling water: {{egy-h|N39}} ({{m|egy|𓈜}}). A form with a few diagonal lines across the interior {{egy-h|N37A}} ({{m|egy|𓈚}}) appeared in hieratic and semi-cursive hieroglyphs during the Old Kingdom but rarely appeared in hieroglyphs proper before the Late Period. Another variant<!--found since at least the Pyramid texts of Unas per Waugh--> adds lines, perhaps to suggest depth: {{egy-h|N38}} ({{m|egy|𓈛}}). In general, the outline and ripples of the glyph are usually colored black or dark blue, the water green or lighter blue (compare the canal glyph {{m|egy|𓈘}} and the ripple glyph {{m|egy|𓈖}}).[[Category:Green Egyptian hieroglyphs]][[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]] The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||š}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|š||pool, lake}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|S}}|tr=š}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||š}}.}}
# {{ng|Occasional [[biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||zn}} in {{m|egy|zn||to open}}, by confusion with {{egy-h|X4I}}, a variant of {{egy-h|X4}} ({{m|egy|𓏒}}).}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|š||pool, lake}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for bodies of water, interchanging with {{egy-h|N36}} ({{m|egy|𓈘}}).}}
# {{ng|Occasional [[determinative]] for irrigated land, by confusion with {{egy-h|N36}} ({{m|egy|𓈘}}).}}
# {{ng|Occasional [[determinative]] for stone, by confusion with {{egy-h|O39}} ({{m|egy|𓊌}}).}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph {{egy-h|S}} and the {{m|egy|jnr||stone}} logogram and determinative {{egy-h|O39}} ({{m|egy|𓊌}}) are extremely similar. The stone hieroglyph is often drawn shorter/squatter than the pool hieroglyph to distinguish the two. In some inscriptions they could also be distinguished by color: the sides of the pool glyph were blue and the interior water was green<!-- were black sides and blue water ever an alternative in period or is that only in later colorings?-->, while the stone glyph was often white<!-- or yellow-->, although sometimes the stone glyph was colored blue instead, see [[𓊌|its entry]] for more.</div><!--ref: Nunn, below-->

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|491}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|13}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

===Further reading===
* Nina Davies, ''Picture Writing in Ancient Egypt'' (1958)

𓈙.references:
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|491}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|13}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a pool with legs. A combination of the hieroglyphs {{egy-h|N37}} ({{m|egy|𓈙}}, phonogram for {{m|egy|š}}) and {{egy-h|D54}} ({{m|egy|𓂻}}, representing {{m|egy|jwj|t=to walk}}).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|Sm}}|tr=šm}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||šm}}, as in {{m|egy|šmj||to go, walk}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|492}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the plan of a [[house]].

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>pr</hiero>|tr=pr}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||pr}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|pr||house}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for buildings and, less commonly, seats and places.}}

====Descendants====
* Proto-Sinaitic: [[Image:Proto-semiticB-01.svg|20px]]
** Proto-Canaanite: [[Image:Proto-Canaanite_-_bet.svg|20px]]
*** {{desctree|phn|𐤁}}
** {{desc|sem-srb|𐩨}}
*** {{desc|gez|በ|tr=b}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a building with no precise identification, sometimes called a courtyard, but possibly a plan of a rudimentary or temporary house, with an L-shaped corridor affording privacy for the interior. Old Kingdom forms generally show the sign taller than it is wide, but in later times it could vary greatly in proportions to fit the space available. {{egy-hiero-color|blue|nodot=y}} or black, likely representing mud brick, with the interior spaces filled in with white.<!-- (Although Gardiner describes the glyph as a "reed shelter", reed would be green.)--> Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|广|sc=Hani}}. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||h}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|h||courtyard<sup>?</sup>}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>h</hiero>|tr=h}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||h}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|h||courtyard<sup>?</sup>}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|493}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|11}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

𓉔.references:
