etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a plan of a walled rectangular enclosure, with the smaller rectangle representing the roofed living quarters.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>Hwt</hiero>|tr=ḥwt}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḥwt}}, as in {{m|egy|ḥwt||verse}}, {{m|egy|ḥwtt||quarry}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḥwt||large building}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|493}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a [[doorleaf]], in the form of a wooden panel with bronze [[flashing]] along one edge, terminating in two cylindrical bronze pins that turned in the hinge. Because this sign was usually accompanied by horizontal signs such as {{m|egy|𓉻}} (<hiero>aA</hiero>), it was typically written in a horizontal orientation as well in order to better fill the available space.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>O31</hiero>|tr=ꜥꜣ}}

# {{lb|egy|rare}} {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ꜥꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ꜥꜣ||doorleaf}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words relating to opening something, as in {{m|egy|wn||to open}}, {{m|egy|zn||to open}}.}}

====Descendants====
* {{desctree|phn|𐤃}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|496}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a façade of a palace or tomb.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>O33</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|Used to mark the name of a king, initially by being written next to it; since the middle Naqada III period, it was instead attached to a rectangular enclosure that contained the royal name, particularly the [[Horus name]].}} {{defdate|Since c. 3400 BCE}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|srḫ||[[serekh]]}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|srḫ||[[serekh]]}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|496}}

===Further reading===
* {{pedia|Serekh}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a door bolt used in double doors. The bulges in the center kept the bolt restrained between two fastening rings on one door; slid from one end to the other, it would pass through a third fastening ring on the other door and so lock it. When summarized, the bulges could be reduced to a pair of rounded dots. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} The phonogrammatic value derives by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|z||door bolt}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>z</hiero>|tr=z, s}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||z}}; after Old Egyptian, this phoneme merged with {{m|egy||s}}, so this glyph became an alternative phonogram for {{m|egy||s}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|z||door bolt; being; type of fish}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫm||Letopolis}}, by confusion with <hiero>R22</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓋉}}).}} {{defdate|since the Middle Kingdom}}

====Usage notes====
Homophonic to <hiero>s</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓋴}}) after Old Egyptian, when the two sounds merged.

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|p=496}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|12}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Originally representing the plan of a fortified brick enclosure with buttresslike projections. Very early this signification was forgotten and the glyph was reinterpreted as a wall. A horizontal variant {{egy-h|O36w}} is also found. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|囗|sc=Hani}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|O36}}}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|jnb||wall}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for walls, as in {{m|egy|wmtt||fortification, bulwark}}, {{m|egy|snb||to overleap (a wall)}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|496}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a cut block of stone or brick. The dimension of the sign varies, and it may be written long like the pool glyph {{m|egy|𓈙}} (<hiero>S</hiero>), with which it should not be confused; the stone glyph was often written shorter to distinguish it. The stone glyph was conventionally colored either white<!-- or yellow--> (in many examples, representing a whitish stone such as limestone) or blue (in other examples, perhaps representing a mud-brick—as Egyptian scribes generally used a limited color palette that did not include grey or brown—or a block of dark stone such as basalt or greywacke, although blue coloration has also been interpreted as mere confusion with the pool glyph).<!--ref: Nunn, below--></div>

Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|石|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>O39</hiero>}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|jnr||stone}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for stone, as in {{m|egy|jnr||stone}}, {{m|egy|ꜥꜣt||precious stone}}, {{m|egy|dbn||deben, a measure of weight}}, {{m|egy|ꜥr||pebble}}, {{m|egy|ḏbt||brick}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph and <hiero>S</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓈙}}) are extremely similar, see the usage notes there regarding typical coloration and other distinguishing features of the two.</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|497}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

