etymologies.etymology_text:
==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a stairway, conventionally with four steps.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>O40</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|rwd||stairway}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḫtw||platform, terrace, terraced hillside}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for stairways and stepped constructions.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|497}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a double stairway.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>O41</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to ascent, as in {{m|egy|jꜥr||to ascend}}, {{m|egy|qꜣy||ascent, high place}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|497}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a round settlement, walled and divided by crossroads. Similar settlements are archaeologically attested in Upper Egypt since the earliest periods of urbanization, and this hieroglyph is also attested very early. In the Old and Middle Kingdoms the streets are occasionally found in a different orientation {{egy-h|O49B}}, horizontal and vertical instead of diagonal; this is even more frequent in inscriptions of the Heracleopolitan Period and 11th dynasty. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|囗|sc=Hani}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|O49}}}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|nwt||town, city}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in names of settlements and inhabited places, as in {{m|egy|kmt||Egypt}}, {{m|egy|wꜣst||Thebes}}, {{m|egy|spꜣt||estate, farm}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|498}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|37}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a circular threshing floor covered with grain kernels. Sometimes it appears as two concentric circles, but more detailed renderings show tiny oblong kernels of grain in the inner circle and green markings on the outer circle. Late forms of the glyph render it as two short diagonal lines inside a circle. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||zp}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a determinative for a threshing floor, {{m|egy|zpt}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>zp</hiero>|tr=zp}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||zp}}, as in {{m|egy|zp||time}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|zpt||threshing floor}}.}}
# {{ng|Used in <hiero>zp:Z1*Z1</hiero>, a [[logogram]] for {{m|egy|zpwj snwj||twice}} that also functions as an [[iteration mark]].}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|498}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a boat on water, typically with seat and steering oar. These boats occur with great frequency in archaic graffiti, pottery, and paintings. In Old Kingdom examples the glyph is usually written without the oar, {{egy-h|P1C}}, and a variant with a high prow and stern, {{egy-h|P30}}, is preferred in Old Kingdom titles.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|P1}}|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|jmw||boat}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|dpt||ship}} in {{m|egy|dpt-nṯr}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ꜥḥꜥ||boat}} in {{m|egy|jmj-r ꜥḥꜥw}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|rare}} {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|qꜣqꜣw||kind of riverboat}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for [[boat]]s and [[ship]]s, as in {{m|egy|dpt||ship}} or {{m|egy|ḥꜥw||fleet}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for verbs involving navigation and travel by water, as in {{m|egy|nꜥj||to travel by boat}} or {{m|egy|ḫdj||to go downstream}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|498}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|37}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a reed mat or a stool of reed matting; presumably the original depiction was a mat. Ordinarily this glyph is taller than it is wide, and, if colored, colored green. Thus, forms from the Old Kingdom often show the details of the weave of the reeds in a bright green. From the Old until the New Kingdom it was occasionally stylized as a rectangle with a shaded left half and unshaded right half. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||p}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|p||stool, socle}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>p</hiero>|tr=p}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||p}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|p||stool, socle}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|500}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|10}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
A picture of a folded cloth. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} The original word behind the phonogrammatic value {{m|egy||s}} is unknown.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>S29</hiero>|tr=s}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||s}}; after Old Egyptian, as {{m|egy||s}} and {{m|egy||z}} merged, it could also be a phonogram for old {{m|egy||z}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|s||vessel of gold}}.}}
# {{ng|Abbreviation for {{m|egy|snb||health}} in the formula {{m|egy|ꜥnḫ wḏꜣ snb||life, prosperity, health}}.}}

====Usage notes====
Homophonic to <hiero>z</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓊃}}) after Old Egyptian, when the two sounds merged.

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|p=507}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an axe. An axe of more recent type, from the 12th Dynasty onward.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for {{m|egy|ꜣqḥw|t=axe}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|511}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing an archer’s bow consisting of two oryx horns joined by a wooden centerpiece. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|弓|sc=Hani}}. The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||pḏ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as the logogram for {{m|egy|pḏt||bow}}.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>T9</hiero>|tr=pḏ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy|pḏ}} or {{q|later}} {{m|egy|pd}}, as in {{m|egy|pḏ||stretch}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|pḏt||bow}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for words relating to bows.}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{q|Old Kingdom}} {{l|egy|𓌓}} (<hiero>T9A</hiero>)

====Descendants====
* {{desc|arc|𐡔‬}}
** {{desc|syc|ܫ}}
*** {{desc|ar|س}}
* {{desc|he|ש‬|tr=s, sh}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|511}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a [[composite bow]] [[currently]] not in use, thus showing the [[middle]] tied to the [[bowstring]].</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>T10</hiero>}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|jwnt||bow}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|pḏt||bow}}.}} {{defdate|since the 12th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|511}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a [[throwstick]] or, in its function representing foreignness, a [[club]] as a [[foreign]] [[weapon]] of war.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>T14</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|qmꜣ||to throw, to create}}.}}
# {{ng|[[phonetic determinative|Phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||ṯn}}, as in {{m|egy|ṯnj||where}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ꜥmꜥꜣt||throwstick}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|qmꜣ||to throw, to create}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for foreign countries and people, as in {{m|egy|ṯḥnw||Libya}}, {{m|egy|ꜥꜣm||Asiatic}}, {{m|egy|nḥsj||Nubian}}.}}
# {{alt form|egy|𓆱}} (<hiero>M3</hiero>) {{ng|as a [[phonetic determinative]] for {{m|egy||ḏꜥ}}, as in {{m|egy|ḏꜥr||to seek out}}.}}
# {{alt form|egy|𓊧}} (<hiero>P11</hiero>) {{ng|in {{m|egy|mjnj||to moor}}.}}
# {{alt form|egy|𓌘}} (<hiero>T13</hiero>)
# {{alt form|egy|𓐨}} (<hiero>Aa26</hiero>) {{ng|in {{m|egy|sbj||rebel}}.}}

====Descendants====
* Proto-Sinaitic: [[Image:Proto-semiticG-01.svg|20px]]
** Proto-Canaanite: [[Image:Protogimel.svg|20px]]
*** {{desctree|phn|𐤂}}
** {{desc|sem-srb|𐩴}}
*** {{desc|gez|ገ}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a one-barbed harpoon.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>T21</hiero>|tr=wꜥ}}

# {{ng|[[biliteral|Biliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||wꜥ}}, including in the word {{m|egy|wꜥ}}.}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing the [[cartouche]] with an architectural rather than mythological image, as a gigantic ceiling resting on four supports. The phonogrammatic value is derived from the glyph’s use in {{m|egy|šnw||cartouche}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>V10</hiero>|tr=šnw}}

# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|šnw||cartouche}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in words for the cartouche, as in {{m|egy|šnw}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|485}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a tethering rope. {{egy-hiero-color|green}} The phonetic value of {{m|egy||ṯ}} is of uncertain derivation.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>T</hiero>|tr=ṯ}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ṯ}}; since Middle Egyptian sometimes used for {{m|egy||t}}, as the sounds had merged in some phonetic environments.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|523}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a lamp wick of twisted flax. The uppermost loop is often the biggest of the three. These wicks were generally made of a folded strip of fabric twisted tightly and soaked in unused fat. {{egy-hiero-color|green}} The phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||ḥ}} is derived by the [[rebus principle]] from {{m|egy|ḥꜥt||wick}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>H</hiero>|tr=ḥ}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḥ}}.}}
# {{lb|egy|rare}} {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|tkꜣw||candle, taper}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|525}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|11}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==
[[File:Hieroglyphs from the tomb of Seti I.jpg|right|thumb|Colorful hieroglyphs from the tomb of Seti I, showing 𓎡.]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing a wickerwork basket with a handle. Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|𠀠|sc=Hani}}, {{m|mul|其|sc=Hani}}. In the Old Kingdom the wickerwork was often detailed as green or yellow horizontal striations, representing a basket made from a continuous spiral, with strips whipstitched together. In later times multicolored checkered patterns became more common. The usual color is green. The phonetic value of {{m|egy||k}} is of uncertain derivation.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>k</hiero>|tr=k}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||k}}.}}

====Descendants====
{| class="wikitable"
|--
! Hieratic
! Demotic
! Coptic
|-
| [[File:Hieratic_V31A_Elephantine_-_Möller.png|40px]] || [[File:Demotic-character-k.png|40px]] || <big><big><big>{{l|cop|Ϭ|tr=-}}</big></big></big>
|}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|525}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|13}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a sealed oil-jar with tied ends, a later development of {{m|egy|𓎰}} (<hiero>W2</hiero>).</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>W1</hiero>|tr=-}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for oil and unguent.}}
# {{alt form|egy|𓎰}} (<hiero>W2</hiero>). {{defdate|since the Middle Kingdom}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|527}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|49–50}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing a sealed oil-jar without tied ends. Old Kingdom forms of this glyph often omit the seal on top and sometimes show alabaster veining.

<div>According to Fischer,<ref>{{R:egy:Calligraphy|49-50}}</ref> since the Middle Kingdom the oil-jar with tied ends <hiero>W1</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓎯}}) has also been used in place of this glyph.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>W2</hiero>|tr=bꜣs}}

# {{ng|[[triliteral|Triliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||bꜣs}}, as in {{m|egy|bꜣstt||(cat-goddess) Bastet}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for jar, as in {{m|egy|bꜣs||jar}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|527}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|49–50}}
<references/>

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
Representing an alabaster bowl used in purifications.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>W3</hiero>|tr=ḥꜣb}}

# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for alabaster, as in {{m|egy|šs||alabaster}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḥꜣb||feast}}.}}
# {{ng|[[phonogram|Phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ḥꜣb}}, as in {{m|egy|ḥꜣbj||to mourn}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for feast, as in {{m|egy|psḏntjw||new-moon festival}}, {{m|egy|wꜣg||wag-festival}}.}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|527}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|50}}

==Egyptian==

===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a ringstand for holding pots and jars. The Old Kingdom version most frequently had a flat bottom and top: <hiero>W12</hiero> ({{l|egy|𓎽}}). The curved form developed later, with rare examples first surfacing during the 6th Dynasty. The body of the glyph was conventionally colored red; the triangle in the center was conventionally white. The reason for its phonogrammatic value of {{m|egy||g}} is unknown.</div>

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head=<hiero>g</hiero>|tr=g}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||g}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|nst||throne}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for or [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|dšrt||red pot}}, by conflation with <hiero>W13</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓎾}}).}}
# {{ng|Occasional [[determinative]] in {{m|egy|jpt||inner chambers}}, by confusion with <hiero>O45</hiero> ({{m|egy|𓊒}}).}} {{defdate|18th Dynasty}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|529}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|14}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)
<!-- [[Category:White Egyptian hieroglyphs]] -->

